window.SUMMIT_READ&&window.SUMMIT_READ("p4",{"part":"<p>Part IV is the professional core of aerospace engineering: how compressible gases flow through nozzles and around vehicles, how rocket engines and motors work and are designed, how spacecraft move under gravity, how launch vehicles fly and are steered, how structures survive, how avionics sense and communicate, and how all of it is organized into a reliable system.</p>","ch":{"23":{"intro":"","secs":{"23.1":"<p>Consider a weak pressure wave moving at speed <span class=\"mi\">a</span> into still gas. In the frame of the wave, gas enters at <span class=\"mi\">a</span> and leaves at <span class=\"mi\">a + du</span> with property changes <span class=\"mi\">dp</span> and <span class=\"mi\">d\\rho</span>. Continuity gives <span class=\"mi\">a\\, d\\rho = - \\rho\\, du</span>, and momentum gives <span class=\"mi\">dp = - \\rho a\\, du</span>. Eliminating <span class=\"mi\">du</span> gives <span class=\"mi\">a^{2} = dp/d\\rho</span>. The wave is infinitesimally weak and adiabatic, hence isentropic:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">a^{2} = \\left( \\frac{\\partial p}{\\partial\\rho} \\right)_{s} = \\frac{\\gamma p}{\\rho} = \\gamma RT</span> (ideal gas). <strong>Mach number</strong> <span class=\"mi\">M = V/a</span>. <strong>Mach angle</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\mu = arcsin(1/M)</span>: in supersonic flow a small disturbance is felt only inside a downstream cone of half-angle <span class=\"mi\">\\mu</span>.</p></div><p>Air at 288 K: <span class=\"mi\">a = 340</span> m/s. Hydrogen at 300 K: about 1320 m/s. Rocket exhaust at 3500 K with <span class=\"mi\">\\mathcal{M} = 13</span> kg/kmol and <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma = 1.2</span>: about 1640 m/s.</p>","23.2":"<p>For adiabatic flow without work, <span class=\"mi\">h_{0} = h + V^{2}/2</span> is constant (Chapter 16). With <span class=\"mi\">h = c_{p}T</span> and <span class=\"mi\">V^{2} = M^{2}\\gamma RT</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">\\frac{T_{0}}{T} = 1 + \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M^{2}</span>, and for isentropic deceleration <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{p_{0}}{p} = \\left( \\frac{T_{0}}{T} \\right)^{\\gamma/(\\gamma - 1)}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{\\rho_{0}}{\\rho} = \\left( \\frac{T_{0}}{T} \\right)^{1/(\\gamma - 1)}</span>. <strong>Sonic state</strong> (<span class=\"mi\">M = 1</span>): <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{T^{*}}{T_{0}} = \\frac{2}{\\gamma + 1}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{p^{*}}{p_{0}} = \\left( \\frac{2}{\\gamma + 1} \\right)^{\\gamma/(\\gamma - 1)}</span> (0.8333 and 0.5283 for γ = 1.4). <strong>Maximum speed</strong> (expansion to 0 K): <span class=\"mi\">V_{\\max} = \\sqrt{2c_{p}T_{0}}</span>.</p></div><p><span class=\"mi\">T_{0}</span> is constant in any adiabatic flow, even through shocks and friction, whereas <span class=\"mi\">p_{0}</span> is constant only in isentropic flow. Expanding <span class=\"mi\">p_{0}/p</span> for small <span class=\"mi\">M</span> gives <span class=\"mi\">p_{0} - p = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho V^{2}\\left( 1 + M^{2}/4 + \\cdots \\right)</span>. Below about <span class=\"mi\">M = 0.3</span>, density changes are under 5% and incompressible analysis suffices.</p>","23.3":"<p>Assume the area <span class=\"mi\">A(x)</span> varies slowly and properties are uniform across each section. Then <span class=\"mi\">\\rho VA = \\text{const}</span> and, for inviscid flow, <span class=\"mi\">dp = - \\rho V\\, dV</span>. Logarithmic differentiation of continuity, combined with <span class=\"mi\">dp = a^{2}d\\rho</span>, gives:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Area–velocity relation:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{dA}{A} = \\left( M^{2} - 1 \\right)\\frac{dV}{V}</span>. Subsonic flow accelerates in a <em>converging</em> duct; supersonic flow accelerates in a <em>diverging</em> duct; <span class=\"mi\">M = 1</span> can occur only where <span class=\"mi\">dA = 0</span>, at a throat. Hence the converging–diverging (de Laval) nozzle.</p></div>","23.4":"<p>Relating any section to the (possibly fictitious) sonic area <span class=\"mi\">A^{*}</span>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{A}{A^{*}} = \\frac{1}{M}\\left\\lbrack \\frac{2}{\\gamma + 1}\\left( 1 + \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M^{2} \\right) \\right\\rbrack^{\\frac{\\gamma + 1}{2(\\gamma - 1)}}.</span></p><p>Every <span class=\"mi\">A/A^{*} &gt; 1</span> has two solutions, one subsonic and one supersonic; which one occurs is set by the downstream conditions. Invert it numerically (Chapter 14) or with Appendix E.</p>","23.5":"<p><span class=\"md\">\\dot{m} = \\frac{p_{0}A}{\\sqrt{RT_{0}}}\\sqrt{\\gamma}\\, M\\left( 1 + \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M^{2} \\right)^{- \\frac{\\gamma + 1}{2(\\gamma - 1)}}.</span></p><p>This is maximal at <span class=\"mi\">M = 1</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Choked flow:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{m} = \\frac{\\Gamma\\, p_{0}A^{*}}{\\sqrt{RT_{0}}}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">\\Gamma = \\sqrt{\\gamma}\\left( \\frac{2}{\\gamma + 1} \\right)^{\\frac{\\gamma + 1}{2(\\gamma - 1)}}</span> (0.6847 for γ = 1.4; 0.6485 for γ = 1.2). Once the throat is sonic, mass flow depends only on upstream stagnation conditions and is independent of downstream pressure. It scales with <span class=\"mi\">p_{0}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">1/\\sqrt{T_{0}}</span>.</p></div><p>This is why rocket chamber pressure is proportional to mass flow (Chapter 26), and why choked orifices meter flow precisely.</p>","23.6":"<p>For a fixed converging–diverging nozzle with reservoir pressure <span class=\"mi\">p_{0}</span>, lowering the back pressure <span class=\"mi\">p_{b}</span> produces this sequence:</p><ol type=\"1\"> <li>Fully subsonic flow, with the minimum pressure at the throat.</li> <li>Throat just sonic; subsonic flow downstream. The mass flow is now fixed.</li> <li>A normal shock stands in the diverging section, with subsonic flow after it. The shock moves toward the exit as <span class=\"mi\">p_{b}</span> falls.</li> <li>The shock reaches the exit plane.</li> <li><em>Overexpanded</em>: supersonic exit flow with <span class=\"mi\">p_{e} &lt; p_{b}</span>, adjusted by oblique shocks outside the nozzle.</li> <li><em>Design condition</em>: <span class=\"mi\">p_{e} = p_{b}</span> (ideally expanded).</li> <li><em>Underexpanded</em>: <span class=\"mi\">p_{e} &gt; p_{b}</span>, adjusted by expansion fans outside.</li> </ol><p>Real overexpanded nozzles do not sustain cases 3–5 as ideal theory predicts: the boundary layer separates when the wall pressure falls to roughly <span class=\"mi\">p_{sep} \\approx (0.25</span>–<span class=\"mi\">0.4)p_{a}</span> (Summerfield criterion, empirical). Separation causes asymmetric <em>side loads</em> during engine start at sea level and limits the area ratio of first-stage nozzles.</p>","23.7":"<p>A supersonic tunnel accelerates gas through a first throat to the test-section Mach number and recovers pressure in a diffuser with a second throat. To <em>start</em> the tunnel, the starting normal shock must pass through the second throat. This requires <span class=\"mi\">A_{t2}/A_{t1} \\geq p_{01}/p_{02}</span>, the stagnation-pressure ratio across a normal shock at the test Mach number. The same physics governs the starting of supersonic inlets (Chapter 44).</p>","23.8":"<p>These results assume a calorically perfect gas with constant <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span>. At high temperatures, vibrational excitation and dissociation change <span class=\"mi\">c_{p}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span> (Chapter 39), and a rocket nozzle’s flow may be frozen or in shifting chemical equilibrium (Chapter 26). In hypersonic blowdown tunnels the air must be heated before expansion so that it does not cool below its condensation temperature in the test section.</p>"}},"24":{"intro":"","secs":{"24.1":"<p>Across a stationary normal shock (a thin, nonequilibrium region a few mean free paths thick), the conservation laws require</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\rho_{1}u_{1} = \\rho_{2}u_{2},\\quad\\quad p_{1} + \\rho_{1}u_{1}^{2} = p_{2} + \\rho_{2}u_{2}^{2},\\quad\\quad h_{1} + \\frac{1}{2}u_{1}^{2} = h_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}u_{2}^{2}.</span></p><p>For a calorically perfect gas, these give the <em>Prandtl relation</em> <span class=\"mi\">u_{1}u_{2} = a^{*2}</span> and:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">M_{2}^{2} = \\frac{1 + \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}M_{1}^{2}}{\\gamma M_{1}^{2} - \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{2}}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}} = 1 + \\frac{2\\gamma}{\\gamma + 1}\\left( M_{1}^{2} - 1 \\right)</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\frac{\\rho_{2}}{\\rho_{1}} = \\frac{(\\gamma + 1)M_{1}^{2}}{2 + (\\gamma - 1)M_{1}^{2}}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} = \\frac{p_{2}/p_{1}}{\\rho_{2}/\\rho_{1}}</span>. Stagnation pressure falls: <span class=\"mi\">p_{02}/p_{01} = e^{- \\Delta s/R}</span>.</p></div><p>The entropy change is positive only for <span class=\"mi\">M_{1} &gt; 1</span>, so only <em>compression</em> shocks exist. For weak shocks <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta s \\propto \\left( M_{1}^{2} - 1 \\right)^{3}</span>: nearly isentropic. As <span class=\"mi\">M_{1} \\rightarrow \\infty</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{2}/\\rho_{1} \\rightarrow (\\gamma + 1)/(\\gamma - 1)</span> (6 for γ = 1.4) while <span class=\"mi\">p_{2}/p_{1}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">T_{2}/T_{1}</span> grow as <span class=\"mi\">M_{1}^{2}</span>. This is the origin of extreme shock-layer temperatures at hypersonic speeds (Chapters 38–39). A Pitot probe in supersonic flow sits behind a normal shock, and the <em>Rayleigh Pitot formula</em> relates its reading to <span class=\"mi\">M_{1}</span>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{p_{02}}{p_{1}} = \\frac{\\left\\lbrack \\frac{\\gamma + 1}{2}M_{1}^{2} \\right\\rbrack^{\\gamma/(\\gamma - 1)}}{\\left\\lbrack \\frac{2\\gamma}{\\gamma + 1}M_{1}^{2} - \\frac{\\gamma - 1}{\\gamma + 1} \\right\\rbrack^{1/(\\gamma - 1)}}.</span></p>","24.2":"<p>A wedge of half-angle <span class=\"mi\">\\theta</span> in supersonic flow produces an attached oblique shock at angle <span class=\"mi\">\\beta</span>. The velocity component tangent to the shock is unchanged, so the normal-shock relations apply to the normal component <span class=\"mi\">M_{n1} = M_{1}\\sin\\beta</span>. Then <span class=\"mi\">M_{2} = M_{n2}/sin(\\beta - \\theta)</span>, and</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\tan\\theta = 2\\cot\\beta\\,\\frac{M_{1}^{2}\\sin^{2}\\beta - 1}{M_{1}^{2}(\\gamma + cos2\\beta) + 2}.</span></p><p>For each <span class=\"mi\">M_{1}</span> there is a maximum deflection <span class=\"mi\">\\theta_{\\max}</span>. Below it, two solutions exist: the <em>weak</em> one (usually observed; <span class=\"mi\">M_{2}</span> mostly supersonic) and the <em>strong</em> one (subsonic <span class=\"mi\">M_{2}</span>). Above <span class=\"mi\">\\theta_{\\max}</span> the shock <em>detaches</em> and stands ahead of the body as a curved bow shock, normal on the axis and weakening to a Mach wave far away (Appendix F tabulates β and θ_max).</p>","24.3":"<p>An oblique shock striking a wall reflects so as to turn the flow back parallel to the wall. If the required turn exceeds <span class=\"mi\">\\theta_{\\max}</span> for the post-shock Mach number, a <em>Mach reflection</em> forms, with a near-normal <em>Mach stem</em>. Shocks crossing each other, or shocks meeting expansion fans, are solved by matching pressure and flow direction across slip lines; the <em>shock polar</em> diagram organizes these solutions. Where a shock meets a boundary layer, it can cause separation (Chapter 37). Where a shock meets another shock near a surface, it can produce extreme local heating (Edney interactions, Chapter 41).</p>","24.4":"<p>Supersonic flow turning around a convex corner expands isentropically through a fan of Mach waves. Each infinitesimal turn obeys <span class=\"mi\">d\\theta = \\sqrt{M^{2} - 1}\\, dV/V</span>. Integrating gives the <em>Prandtl–Meyer function</em>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">\\nu(M) = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma + 1}{\\gamma - 1}}\\arctan\\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma - 1}{\\gamma + 1}\\left( M^{2} - 1 \\right)} - \\arctan\\sqrt{M^{2} - 1}</span>, with a turn of <span class=\"mi\">\\theta</span> giving <span class=\"mi\">\\nu\\left( M_{2} \\right) = \\nu\\left( M_{1} \\right) + \\theta</span>. The maximum turning (expansion to vacuum) is <span class=\"mi\">\\nu_{\\max} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{\\gamma + 1}{\\gamma - 1}} - 1 \\right) = {130.45}^{\\circ}</span> for γ = 1.4.</p></div>","24.5":"<p>Combining exact oblique shocks (compression corners) with Prandtl–Meyer expansions (expansion corners) gives exact inviscid 2-D solutions for sharp-edged shapes, provided no wave reflections reach the surface. Applied to a flat plate at incidence <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span>, the lower surface is compressed by a shock turning the flow by <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span>, and the upper surface expands through <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span>. Lift and wave drag follow from integrating the surface pressures. Chapter 25 compares this exact result with linear (Ackeret) theory.</p>","24.6":"<p>Adiabatic flow with wall friction in a constant-area duct conserves <span class=\"mi\">h_{0}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\rho u</span> while entropy rises. Friction always drives the Mach number toward 1: subsonic flow accelerates and supersonic flow decelerates. The duct length that brings Mach <span class=\"mi\">M</span> to sonic is</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{4\\bar{f}L^{*}}{D} = \\frac{1 - M^{2}}{\\gamma M^{2}} + \\frac{\\gamma + 1}{2\\gamma}\\ln\\frac{(\\gamma + 1)M^{2}}{2 + (\\gamma - 1)M^{2}},</span></p><p>with <span class=\"mi\">\\bar{f}</span> the mean Fanning friction factor. A longer duct chokes, and the upstream flow must adjust: subsonic flow reduces its mass flow; supersonic flow forms a shock.</p>","24.7":"<p>Frictionless constant-area flow with heat addition <span class=\"mi\">q</span> changes the stagnation temperature, <span class=\"mi\">q = c_{p}\\left( T_{02} - T_{01} \\right)</span>. Heating also drives <span class=\"mi\">M</span> toward 1: it decelerates supersonic flow (raising <span class=\"mi\">p</span> and <span class=\"mi\">T</span>) and accelerates subsonic flow (lowering <span class=\"mi\">p</span>).</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">\\frac{T_{0}}{T_{0}^{*}} = \\frac{(\\gamma + 1)M^{2}\\left\\lbrack 2 + (\\gamma - 1)M^{2} \\right\\rbrack}{\\left( 1 + \\gamma M^{2} \\right)^{2}}</span>. <strong>Thermal choking:</strong> the most heat a constant-area duct can accept raises <span class=\"mi\">T_{0}</span> to <span class=\"mi\">T_{0}^{*}</span>. Adding more forces the upstream flow to change: in a supersonic combustor, a shock system (or <em>unstart</em>) results. Scramjet combustors therefore diverge in area (Chapter 44).</p></div><p>In subsonic Rayleigh flow the static temperature peaks at <span class=\"mi\">M = 1/\\sqrt{\\gamma}</span>, before choking: heating there lowers <span class=\"mi\">T</span> while raising <span class=\"mi\">T_{0}</span>.</p>","24.8":"<p>Steady, irrotational, 2-D supersonic flow is hyperbolic (Chapter 12). Its characteristics are the Mach lines, at angles <span class=\"mi\">\\theta \\pm \\mu</span> to the flow. Along them the Riemann invariants are constant: <span class=\"mi\">\\theta + \\nu = K_{-}</span> along <span class=\"mi\">C_{-}</span> lines and <span class=\"mi\">\\theta - \\nu = K_{+}</span> along <span class=\"mi\">C_{+}</span> lines. Unit processes (interior point, wall point, symmetry point) march the solution through a network of characteristics. A <em>minimum-length nozzle</em> expands the flow at a sharp throat corner of angle <span class=\"mi\">\\nu_{e}/2</span> and cancels all waves on the contoured wall to deliver uniform exit flow. Rocket bell nozzles are designed with characteristics plus an optimization of the thrust for a given length (the Rao method).</p>"}},"25":{"intro":"","secs":{"25.1":"<p>The resultant aerodynamic force splits into lift <span class=\"mi\">L</span> (perpendicular to the free stream) and drag <span class=\"mi\">D</span> (parallel to it), or, in body axes, normal force <span class=\"mi\">N</span> and axial force <span class=\"mi\">A</span>. Coefficients: <span class=\"mi\">C_{L} = L/\\left( q_{\\infty}S \\right)</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{D} = D/\\left( q_{\\infty}S \\right)</span>, <span class=\"mi\">C_{m} = M/\\left( q_{\\infty}S\\bar{c} \\right)</span>, with reference area <span class=\"mi\">S</span> and length <span class=\"mi\">\\bar{c}</span> (always state which). The <em>center of pressure</em> is where the resultant acts and moves with angle of attack. The <em>aerodynamic center</em> is the point about which <span class=\"mi\">C_{m}</span> does not vary with <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span>: the quarter chord in thin-airfoil theory and roughly mid-chord in supersonic flow.</p>","25.2":"<p>For incompressible irrotational flow, <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{u} = \\nabla\\phi</span> with <span class=\"mi\">\\nabla^{2}\\phi = 0</span> (Chapter 12), so elementary solutions superpose. A uniform stream plus a doublet gives flow past a cylinder. Adding a vortex of circulation <span class=\"mi\">\\Gamma</span> creates lift:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Kutta–Joukowski theorem:</strong> lift per unit span <span class=\"mi\">L' = \\rho_{\\infty}V_{\\infty}\\Gamma</span>. <strong>Kutta condition:</strong> at a sharp trailing edge, the flow leaves smoothly. This physical condition, enforced by viscosity, fixes the circulation. Inviscid potential flow predicts zero drag (d’Alembert’s paradox): real drag comes from viscosity, waves and trailing vortices.</p></div>","25.3":"<p>Representing the camber line by a vortex sheet and enforcing flow tangency gives, for small <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">c_{l} = 2\\pi\\left( \\alpha - \\alpha_{L = 0} \\right),\\quad\\quad c_{m,c/4} = \\text{const}\\ \\left( \\text{zero for a symmetric section} \\right).</span></p><p>The lift-curve slope is <span class=\"mi\">2\\pi</span> per radian (about 0.11 per degree). Real airfoils stall when the boundary layer separates, typically at <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span> around 12–16°.</p>","25.4":"<p>Trailing vortices induce downwash that tilts the local lift backward, creating <em>induced drag</em>. Prandtl’s lifting-line theory gives</p><p><span class=\"md\">C_{D,i} = \\frac{C_{L}^{2}}{\\pi e\\, AR},\\quad\\quad a = \\frac{a_{0}}{1 + a_{0}/(\\pi e\\, AR)},</span></p><p>with aspect ratio <span class=\"mi\">AR = b^{2}/S</span> and span efficiency <span class=\"mi\">e \\leq 1</span> (<span class=\"mi\">e = 1</span> for an elliptical lift distribution). The drag polar <span class=\"mi\">C_{D} = C_{D0} + kC_{L}^{2}</span> gives a maximum lift-to-drag ratio <span class=\"mi\">(L/D)_{\\max} = 1/\\left( 2\\sqrt{C_{D0}k} \\right)</span> at <span class=\"mi\">C_{L} = \\sqrt{C_{D0}/k}</span>, where induced drag equals parasite drag.</p>","25.5":"<p>Linearizing the compressible potential equation gives <span class=\"mi\">\\left( 1 - M_{\\infty}^{2} \\right)\\phi_{xx} + \\phi_{yy} = 0</span>. Stretching coordinates reduces it to Laplace’s equation, which yields the <em>Prandtl–Glauert rule</em> <span class=\"mi\">C_{p} = C_{p0}/\\sqrt{1 - M_{\\infty}^{2}}</span> (more accurate corrections: Kármán–Tsien, Laitone). The <em>critical Mach number</em> is the free-stream value at which the flow first reaches <span class=\"mi\">M = 1</span> somewhere on the body. Above it, shocks form and drag rises sharply at the <em>drag-divergence</em> Mach number. Supercritical airfoils flatten the upper surface to weaken these shocks. <em>Sweep</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\Lambda</span> reduces the effective normal Mach number to <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty}\\cos\\Lambda</span>, delaying both phenomena.</p>","25.6":"<p>Linearized supersonic theory (Ackeret) gives the surface pressure directly from the local flow inclination <span class=\"mi\">\\theta</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">C_{p} = \\frac{2\\theta}{\\sqrt{M_{\\infty}^{2} - 1}}</span>, so for a flat plate <span class=\"mi\">c_{l} = \\frac{4\\alpha}{\\sqrt{M_{\\infty}^{2} - 1}}</span> and the wave drag is <span class=\"mi\">c_{d,w} = \\frac{4\\alpha^{2}}{\\sqrt{M_{\\infty}^{2} - 1}}</span>. Thickness adds <span class=\"mi\">c_{d,w,t} = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{M_{\\infty}^{2} - 1}}\\overline{\\left( \\frac{dy}{dx} \\right)^{2}}</span>, averaged over both surfaces. The aerodynamic center moves to mid-chord.</p></div><p>Wave drag, which is absent in subsonic flow, makes thin sections and slender bodies mandatory at supersonic speed. The linear theory fails as <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty} \\rightarrow 1</span> (transonic flow) and at high <span class=\"mi\">M_{\\infty}\\theta</span> (hypersonic flow, Chapter 38).</p>","25.7":"<p>Slender-body theory gives a normal-force slope of <span class=\"mi\">C_{N\\alpha} = 2</span> per radian for a pointed body of revolution (based on base area), independent of Mach number. Zero-lift wave drag depends on how the cross-sectional area <span class=\"mi\">S(x)</span> is distributed along the length. The Sears–Haack body minimizes it for a given length and volume. The <em>area rule</em> (Whitcomb) states that near Mach 1 the wave drag of a whole configuration approximates that of a body of revolution with the same area distribution, so aircraft fuselages are “waisted” where the wings attach.</p>","25.8":"<p>A launcher’s aerodynamics are summarized by the axial-force coefficient <span class=\"mi\">C_{A}(M)</span> (peaking near Mach 1–1.2), the normal-force slope <span class=\"mi\">C_{N\\alpha}(M)</span>, and the CP location. Noses and flares contribute most of the normal force; long cylinders contribute little at small α. Base drag at the aft end depends on plume interactions. The <em>Barrowman method</em>, a sum of component contributions, gives quick CP estimates for slender rockets. Transonic flow causes buffet and shock oscillation over shoulders and protuberances. Products of dynamic pressure and angle of attack (<span class=\"mi\">q\\alpha</span>, in kPa·deg) are the standard measure of aerodynamic bending load (Chapter 31).</p>","25.9":"<p>A vehicle is statically stable in pitch if <span class=\"mi\">C_{m\\alpha} &lt; 0</span> about its CG, i.e. the CP of the incremental lift lies behind the CG. The <em>static margin</em> is the distance between them divided by a reference length (calibers for rockets, chord for aircraft). <em>Trim</em> requires <span class=\"mi\">C_{m} = 0</span> at the flight condition, achieved by control surfaces or engine gimballing. More stability means more control power is needed to maneuver. An unstable vehicle needs active control (Chapters 22 and 32).</p>","25.10":"<p>For steady level cruise, <span class=\"mi\">L = W</span> and <span class=\"mi\">T = D</span>. The Breguet range equation follows from integrating <span class=\"mi\">dR = V\\, dt</span> with fuel flow <span class=\"mi\">{\\dot{m}}_{f}g_{0} = T/I_{sp}</span>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">R = V\\, I_{sp}\\,\\frac{L}{D}\\ln\\frac{W_{i}}{W_{f}},</span></p><p>with <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp}</span> in seconds defined on fuel flow. The product <span class=\"mi\">V\\, I_{sp}\\,(L/D)</span> is the figure of merit that hypersonic cruise vehicles must maximize (Chapter 43).</p>"}},"26":{"intro":"","secs":{"26.1":"<p>Draw a control volume around the vehicle, cutting the jet at the nozzle exit plane, and work in the vehicle’s frame at steady state. Outside the vehicle, ambient pressure <span class=\"mi\">p_{a}</span> acts everywhere except over the exit area, where the jet’s pressure <span class=\"mi\">p_{e}</span> acts instead. The momentum balance (Chapter 18) along the axis gives:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">F = \\dot{m}u_{e} + \\left( p_{e} - p_{a} \\right)A_{e}</span>, assuming uniform, axial exit flow. <strong>Effective exhaust velocity</strong> <span class=\"mi\">c = F/\\dot{m} = u_{e} + \\left( p_{e} - p_{a} \\right)A_{e}/\\dot{m}</span>. <strong>Specific impulse</strong> <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp} = c/g_{0}</span>. Vacuum thrust (<span class=\"mi\">p_{a} = 0</span>) is the largest; sea-level thrust is smaller by <span class=\"mi\">p_{a}A_{e}</span>.</p></div>","26.2":"<p>Assumptions: homogeneous perfect gas of fixed composition; adiabatic, frictionless, steady flow; negligible chamber velocity; one-dimensional axial exit. Then the steady-flow energy equation gives <span class=\"mi\">u_{e} = \\sqrt{2c_{p}\\left( T_{c} - T_{e} \\right)}</span>, and with isentropic expansion:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">u_{e} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\gamma}{\\gamma - 1}\\frac{R_{u}T_{c}}{\\mathcal{M}}\\left\\lbrack 1 - \\left( \\frac{p_{e}}{p_{c}} \\right)^{(\\gamma - 1)/\\gamma} \\right\\rbrack}</span>. Exhaust velocity rises with <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{T_{c}\\mathcal{/M}}</span> and with the expansion pressure ratio, approaching <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{2\\gamma R_{u}T_{c}/\\left\\lbrack (\\gamma - 1)\\mathcal{M} \\right\\rbrack}</span> for infinite expansion.</p></div>","26.3":"<p>Separate the <em>chamber</em> (combustion quality) from the <em>nozzle</em> (expansion quality):</p><p><span class=\"md\">c^{*} \\equiv \\frac{p_{c}A_{t}}{\\dot{m}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\gamma RT_{c}}}{\\gamma\\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{2}{\\gamma + 1} \\right)^{(\\gamma + 1)/(\\gamma - 1)}}},\\quad\\quad C_{F} \\equiv \\frac{F}{p_{c}A_{t}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\gamma^{2}}{\\gamma - 1}\\left( \\frac{2}{\\gamma + 1} \\right)^{\\frac{\\gamma + 1}{\\gamma - 1}}\\left\\lbrack 1 - \\left( \\frac{p_{e}}{p_{c}} \\right)^{\\frac{\\gamma - 1}{\\gamma}} \\right\\rbrack} + \\frac{p_{e} - p_{a}}{p_{c}}\\frac{A_{e}}{A_{t}},</span></p><p>with <span class=\"mi\">c = c^{*}C_{F}</span>. <span class=\"mi\">c^{*}</span> depends only on propellant thermochemistry (<span class=\"mi\">T_{c}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\mathcal{M}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span>); measured <span class=\"mi\">c^{*}</span> divided by theoretical <span class=\"mi\">c^{*}</span> is the <em>combustion efficiency</em> (typically 0.95–0.99). <span class=\"mi\">C_{F}</span> depends only on <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span>, the area ratio <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon = A_{e}/A_{t}</span> (which fixes <span class=\"mi\">p_{e}/p_{c}</span> through the area–Mach relation) and <span class=\"mi\">p_{a}/p_{c}</span>. Its vacuum, infinite-expansion limit is about 2.25 for γ = 1.2. Chamber pressure therefore follows directly from mass flow: <span class=\"mi\">p_{c} = \\dot{m}c^{*}/A_{t}</span>.</p>","26.4":"<p>At fixed chamber conditions, <span class=\"mi\">dF/dA_{e} = \\left( p_{e} - p_{a} \\right)</span>, so thrust at a given altitude is maximized when <span class=\"mi\">p_{e} = p_{a}</span> (<em>optimum expansion</em>). A fixed nozzle is therefore ideal at one altitude only. First stages compromise between sea level (where large <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon</span> separates) and altitude; upper stages use very large <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon</span>. Altitude-compensating concepts include aerospikes, dual-bell nozzles and extendible nozzle skirts. Real nozzles lose performance through:</p><ul> <li><strong>Divergence:</strong> a conical nozzle of half-angle <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span> has momentum efficiency <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda = (1 + cos\\alpha)/2</span> (0.983 at 15°). Contoured “bell” nozzles (Rao) recover most of this in shorter lengths.</li> <li><strong>Boundary layers:</strong> about 0.5–1.5% of thrust.</li> <li><strong>Chemical kinetics:</strong> recombination may freeze during rapid expansion (§26.5).</li> <li><strong>Two-phase flow:</strong> condensed particles (for example aluminum oxide in solid motors) lag the gas in velocity and temperature.</li> <li><strong>Nonuniform mixture</strong> from injector maldistribution.</li> </ul><p>Overall, delivered <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp}</span> is typically 92–98% of ideal.</p>","26.5":"<p>As gas expands and cools, dissociated species recombine and release chemical energy. The <em>shifting-equilibrium</em> assumption (instantaneous recombination) gives the upper bound on <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp}</span>; <em>frozen</em> flow (composition fixed at the chamber or throat) gives the lower bound, typically a few percent less. Reality lies between, and finite-rate kinetics calculations locate it. Codes such as NASA CEA compute equilibrium chamber composition by Gibbs minimization (Chapter 17) and both expansion limits.</p>","26.6":"<p>Define the structural coefficient <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon_{s} = m_{s}/\\left( m_{s} + m_{p} \\right)</span> (dry structure and engines over stage mass excluding payload) and the payload ratio <span class=\"mi\">\\pi = m_{pl}/m_{0}</span>. A single stage’s mass ratio is</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{m_{0}}{m_{f}} = \\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_{s} + \\left( 1 - \\varepsilon_{s} \\right)\\pi}.</span></p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>The single-stage limit.</strong> Even with zero payload, <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta v_{\\max} = c\\ln\\left( 1/\\varepsilon_{s} \\right)</span>. With <span class=\"mi\">c = 2942</span> m/s and <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon_{s} = 0.10</span> this is 6.8 km/s, less than the about 9.4 km/s needed for low Earth orbit. Reaching orbit with a single stage requires very low <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon_{s}</span> and high <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp}</span> simultaneously. Staging discards dead mass so that later stages accelerate only what remains.</p></div>","26.7":"<p>Total <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta v = \\sum c_{i}\\ln n_{i}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">n_{i}</span> is the mass ratio of stage <span class=\"mi\">i</span>. Maximizing the overall payload ratio <span class=\"mi\">\\prod\\pi_{i}</span> for a given total Δv with a Lagrange multiplier <span class=\"mi\">\\eta</span> (Chapter 9) gives</p><p><span class=\"md\">n_{i} = \\frac{c_{i}\\eta - 1}{c_{i}\\varepsilon_{i}\\eta},\\quad\\quad\\text{with }\\eta\\text{ chosen so that }\\sum c_{i}\\ln n_{i} = \\Delta v.</span></p><p>For identical stages this reduces to an equal Δv split. Returns diminish beyond three or four stages, while cost and failure modes grow. Parallel staging (boosters around a core) adds thrust at liftoff, and cross-feed propellant transfer between stages is a further refinement.</p>","26.8":"<p><span class=\"md\">\\Delta v_{required} = v_{orbit} + \\Delta v_{gravity} + \\Delta v_{drag} + \\Delta v_{steering} - \\Delta v_{rotation}.</span></p><p>Typical LEO values: orbital speed 7.7–7.8 km/s; gravity loss 1.0–1.5 km/s (Chapter 31); drag loss 0.1–0.3 km/s; steering loss 0.05–0.2 km/s; Earth rotation credit up to 0.465 km/s (eastward from the equator). The total is about 9.3–10 km/s, with each term depending on the trajectory and thrust-to-weight ratio.</p>","26.9":"<p>A stage limited by tank <em>volume</em> rather than mass favors dense propellants. The bulk density at mixture ratio <span class=\"mi\">r = O/F</span> is <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{b} = (1 + r)/\\left( r/\\rho_{o} + 1/\\rho_{f} \\right)</span>. Density-specific impulse <span class=\"mi\">\\rho_{b}I_{sp}</span> is a useful first-stage figure of merit.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Combination (typical <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{O}\\mathbf{/}\\mathbf{F}</span>)</th> <th>Vacuum <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{I}_{\\mathbf{sp}}</span>, representative engines (s)</th> <th>Bulk density (kg/m³)</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>LOX/LH₂ (5.5–6)</td> <td>450–465</td> <td>≈ 360</td> </tr> <tr> <td>LOX/CH₄ (3.3–3.8)</td> <td>350–380</td> <td>≈ 830</td> </tr> <tr> <td>LOX/RP-1 (2.3–2.7)</td> <td>310–350</td> <td>≈ 1020</td> </tr> <tr> <td>NTO/MMH (≈ 2)</td> <td>310–330</td> <td>≈ 1190</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Composite solid (Al/AP/polymer)</td> <td>265–295</td> <td>≈ 1750–1800</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Values depend on chamber pressure, mixture ratio and area ratio. Hydrogen’s high <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp}</span> comes with very large, cold, insulated tanks; that is why many vehicles use dense propellants in the first stage and hydrogen above. Cost, reusability (soot from kerosene versus clean methane), storability and operations also drive the choice.</p>"}},"27":{"intro":"","secs":{"27.1":"<p>A liquid rocket engine comprises propellant tanks with a pressurization system (helium, or autogenous: vaporized propellant), a feed system (pressure-fed or pump-fed), valves and lines, an injector, a thrust chamber (combustion chamber and nozzle) with its cooling system, an igniter, controls, and usually a gimbal for thrust-vector control.</p>","27.2":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Propellant</th> <th>Normal boiling point (K)</th> <th>Density (kg/m³)</th> <th>Notes</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>LOX</td> <td>90.2</td> <td>1141</td> <td>Cryogenic; strong oxidizer; materials must be oxygen-compatible</td> </tr> <tr> <td>LH₂</td> <td>20.3</td> <td>70.8</td> <td>Deep cryogen; very low density; embrittles some metals</td> </tr> <tr> <td>LCH₄</td> <td>111.7</td> <td>423</td> <td>Cryogenic; clean-burning; good regenerative coolant</td> </tr> <tr> <td>RP-1 (kerosene)</td> <td>≈ 450 (range)</td> <td>≈ 810</td> <td>Storable; dense; can coke in hot cooling channels</td> </tr> <tr> <td>NTO (N₂O₄)</td> <td>294</td> <td>1440</td> <td>Storable; toxic; hypergolic with hydrazines</td> </tr> <tr> <td>MMH</td> <td>361</td> <td>874</td> <td>Storable; toxic; hypergolic with NTO</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Hydrazine (N₂H₄)</td> <td>387</td> <td>1004</td> <td>Storable; toxic; also used as a monopropellant</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Cryogens boil off and need insulation, chilldown before engine start, and careful pressurization. Subcooling (“densifying”) cryogens below their boiling point raises density and loads more propellant. Hypergolic pairs ignite reliably on contact but are highly toxic and require specialized handling.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Liquid-engine testing combines toxic or cryogenic propellants, high pressure and ignition. Failures include hard starts (accumulated propellant igniting at once), oxygen-fed fires and combustion instability that can destroy hardware within milliseconds (§27.8–27.11).</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> These failure modes are why engine tests are run remotely from hardened test stands with written procedures, clear zones and emergency plans.</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Detailed injector, igniter and test-stand construction guidance.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> University liquid-rocket student teams and research laboratories operating under faculty supervision and institutional safety review; industry internships and co-ops; professional short courses (for example AIAA continuing-education courses on liquid rocket propulsion); Sutton and Biblarz and Huzel and Huang for the open engineering principles (Appendix K).</p></aside>","27.3":"<p><strong>Pressure-fed:</strong> tank pressure must exceed <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span> plus injector, line and cooling-jacket losses. It is simple and reliable but limits <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span> to roughly 1–3 MPa and makes the tanks heavy, since tank mass scales with <span class=\"mi\">pV</span> (Chapter 20). It is used on upper stages and spacecraft. <strong>Pump-fed:</strong> turbopumps raise the pressure, so tanks stay at a few bar. The turbine’s drive gas defines the cycle:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Cycle</th> <th>Turbine drive</th> <th>Exhaust of turbine</th> <th>Character</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Gas generator (open)</td> <td>Small fraction of propellant burned off-ratio</td> <td>Dumped overboard</td> <td>Simple; Isp penalty of order 1–3%</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Staged combustion (closed)</td> <td>Preburner burns one propellant with a little of the other</td> <td>Injected into main chamber</td> <td>Highest <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span> (20–30 MPa) and Isp; very high pump pressures; oxidizer-rich, fuel-rich or full-flow variants</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Expander (closed or bleed)</td> <td>Fuel heated in the cooling jacket</td> <td>Into chamber (or dumped, bleed)</td> <td>Clean and benign; thrust limited by available heat</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Electric pump</td> <td>Battery-powered motors</td> <td>None</td> <td>Simple; battery mass limits burn time and size</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Tap-off</td> <td>Hot gas tapped from chamber</td> <td>Dumped</td> <td>Few components; demanding turbine environment</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Power balance.</strong> Pump power: <span class=\"mi\">P_{p} = \\dot{m}\\,\\Delta p/\\left( \\rho\\eta_{p} \\right)</span>. Turbine power: <span class=\"mi\">P_{t} = {\\dot{m}}_{t}c_{p}T_{in}\\eta_{t}\\left\\lbrack 1 - \\left( p_{out}/p_{in} \\right)^{(\\gamma - 1)/\\gamma} \\right\\rbrack</span>. The cycle closes when <span class=\"mi\">P_{t} = \\sum P_{p}</span> plus bearing and seal losses. Low-density liquid hydrogen needs about 16 times the pump power of LOX per unit mass for the same pressure rise. In closed cycles, the preburner must exceed <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span> by the turbine pressure ratio, so pump discharge pressures reach 1.5–2 times <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span>.</p></div><p>The expander cycle’s limit is geometric. Heat pickup scales with chamber surface area (<span class=\"mi\">\\propto L^{2}</span>) while the required pump power scales with flow (<span class=\"mi\">\\propto L^{3}</span>), so pure expander engines are limited to moderate thrust.</p>","27.4":"<p>Pump head <span class=\"mi\">H = \\Delta p/(\\rho g)</span>. Euler’s turbomachinery equation gives the specific work as <span class=\"mi\">w = U_{2}c_{\\theta 2} - U_{1}c_{\\theta 1}</span> (blade speed times swirl velocity), so the head per stage is limited by tip speed and therefore by material strength. The dimensionless <em>specific speed</em> <span class=\"mi\">N_{s} = \\omega\\sqrt{Q}/(gH)^{3/4}</span> selects the pump type: radial (low <span class=\"mi\">N_{s}</span>), mixed or axial (high <span class=\"mi\">N_{s}</span>). Hydrogen pumps need enormous head and therefore very high tip speeds or several stages.</p><p><em>Cavitation:</em> if the local pressure falls below the vapor pressure, vapor bubbles form and collapse, eroding blades and cutting head. The net positive suction head available, <span class=\"mi\">NPSH_{a} = \\left( p_{in} - p_{v} \\right)/(\\rho g) + V^{2}/(2g)</span>, must exceed the pump’s requirement. Axial <em>inducers</em> ahead of the main impeller tolerate some cavitation, letting tanks run at low pressure (which saves tank mass). Turbines are <em>impulse</em> types (pressure drop in the nozzles, suited to high pressure ratios) or <em>reaction</em> types. Bearings, dynamic seals (with an inert purge separating oxidizer from fuel) and rotordynamics (critical speeds, whirl) are frequent sources of development failures.</p>","27.5":"<p>An injector meters, atomizes, mixes and distributes propellants, and provides stable combustion and a wall environment the chamber can survive. Each orifice obeys <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{m} = C_{d}A\\sqrt{2\\rho\\Delta p}</span>. An injector pressure drop of about 15–30% of <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span> isolates the feed system from chamber pressure oscillations. Element types:</p><ul> <li><strong>Impinging jets</strong> (unlike doublets, like doublets, triplets): common with storables and kerosene.</li> <li><strong>Shear coaxial:</strong> a central LOX post with an annulus of high-velocity gaseous hydrogen; mixing relies on the velocity ratio.</li> <li><strong>Swirl coaxial:</strong> used widely in oxidizer-rich staged-combustion engines.</li> <li><strong>Pintle:</strong> a single central element that throttles deeply and resists instability.</li> </ul><p>Peripheral elements often run fuel-rich (film cooling) to protect the chamber wall.</p>","27.6":"<p>The throat area follows from <span class=\"mi\">A_{t} = \\dot{m}c^{*}/p_{c}</span>. The <em>characteristic length</em> <span class=\"mi\">L^{*} = V_{c}/A_{t}</span> measures the volume available for vaporization, mixing and reaction. The residence time is <span class=\"mi\">t_{s} = \\rho_{c}V_{c}/\\dot{m} = L^{*}c^{*}/\\left( RT_{c} \\right)</span>, typically a few milliseconds. Typical <span class=\"mi\">L^{*}</span> is of order 0.8–1.3 m, depending on propellants and injector quality. The contraction ratio <span class=\"mi\">A_{c}/A_{t}</span> is about 2–5: larger ratios lower the chamber Mach number and the associated stagnation-pressure loss but add mass and wetted area.</p>","27.7":"<p>Heat flux peaks just upstream of the throat (Bartz, Chapter 19) and rises roughly linearly with <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span>. It reaches tens of MW/m² and exceeds 100 MW/m² in the highest-pressure engines. Methods:</p><ul> <li><strong>Regenerative:</strong> propellant (usually fuel) flows through channels in the wall before injection. Copper-alloy liners with milled channels, closed out by electroformed nickel or a brazed jacket, keep the hot wall below about 800 K. The cyclic hot-wall compressive yielding causes low-cycle thermal fatigue (“doghousing”), the life limiter of reusable chambers. Hydrocarbon coolants can coke; supercritical coolants show property spikes (Chapter 19).</li> <li><strong>Film and transpiration:</strong> coolant injected along or through the wall forms a protective layer, at some cost in Isp.</li> <li><strong>Ablative:</strong> silica- or carbon-phenolic liners char and erode. They are cheap and simple, but limited in duration and throat-erosion tolerance.</li> <li><strong>Radiative:</strong> high-temperature walls (niobium alloys, rhenium/iridium, carbon–carbon, C/SiC) reject heat by radiation (Chapter 19). Used for nozzle extensions and small thrusters.</li> </ul>","27.8":"<p>Oscillations are classified by frequency and mechanism. <strong>Low frequency (chugging):</strong> coupling between chamber pressure and feed-system flow, cured by higher injector Δp or feed-line accumulators. <strong>Intermediate (buzz):</strong> feed-system acoustics or entropy waves. <strong>High frequency (screech):</strong> coupling between chamber acoustic modes and combustion. It is the most destructive, able to destroy an injector face within milliseconds.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Chamber acoustic modes</strong> (closed cylinder of radius <span class=\"mi\">R</span> and length <span class=\"mi\">L</span>): <span class=\"mi\">f = \\frac{a}{2}\\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{\\alpha_{mn}}{\\pi R} \\right)^{2} + \\left( \\frac{q}{L} \\right)^{2}}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha_{10} = 1.8412</span> (first tangential), <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha_{20} = 3.0542</span> (second tangential), <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha_{01} = 3.8317</span> (first radial). <strong>Rayleigh criterion:</strong> oscillations grow if heat-release fluctuations are in phase with pressure fluctuations, <span class=\"mi\">\\oint p'q'\\, dt &gt; 0</span>, by more than the acoustic losses.</p></div><p>Remedies include injector redesign (changing the combustion response time), injector-face <em>baffles</em> that block transverse modes, <em>acoustic cavities</em> or Helmholtz resonators that absorb energy, and higher injector Δp. Stability is demonstrated by <em>bomb tests</em>: small charges perturb the chamber, and the decay of the disturbance is measured. The F-1 engine of the Saturn V needed a long campaign of such full-scale tests before it was declared stable. Chapter 47 develops the theory.</p>","27.9":"<p>Ignition sources: spark torches or augmented spark igniters (common for LOX/LH₂); pyrophoric slugs (triethylaluminum–triethylborane for LOX/kerosene); pyrotechnic igniters; hypergolic contact. Start sequences chill down cryogenic pumps, lead with one propellant to avoid hard starts (accumulated unburned mixture igniting at once), and bootstrap the turbopump. Throttling is limited by injector Δp, which falls as <span class=\"mi\">{\\dot{m}}^{2}</span> and with it stability margin; by pump stall; and by turbine matching. Pintle and variable-area injectors extend the range. Restart requires repeatable ignition and settled propellants (ullage settling burns in weightlessness).</p>","27.10":"<p>Engine design balances the cycle (pressures, temperatures, flows), mass (thrust-to-weight ranges from about 50 to over 150), reliability, cost and, increasingly, reusability: turbine life, oxygen-compatibility margins, inspection access and chamber fatigue life. Development proceeds through component tests (injector, preburner, turbopump), engine hot-fire tests, and qualification beyond flight conditions.</p>","27.11":"<p>Ullage pressure must satisfy the pumps’ suction requirement (NPSH) and stabilize thin tank walls (Chapter 33). It is supplied by stored helium (often chilled and warmed through heat exchangers) or <em>autogenously</em>, by vaporized propellant tapped from the engine (gaseous oxygen, hydrogen or methane). For a helium bottle expanding adiabatically from <span class=\"mi\">p_{0}</span> to a final <span class=\"mi\">p_{f}</span> while expelling propellant volume <span class=\"mi\">V_{t}</span> at tank pressure <span class=\"mi\">p_{t}</span>, the required pressurant mass is</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">m_{g} = \\frac{p_{t}V_{t}}{RT_{0}} \\cdot \\frac{\\gamma}{1 - p_{f}/p_{0}}</span> (adiabatic bottle); replace γ by 1 for an isothermal bottle. Heat transfer and condensation in cryogenic tanks (<em>ullage collapse</em>) increase the real requirement, which is measured in tests.</p></div><p>In weightlessness, propellant must be positioned over the outlet before an engine start, either by a small <em>settling</em> burn or by <em>propellant management devices</em> (surface-tension vanes, screens and sponges, or bladders and diaphragms for storables). Feed lines bring their own hazards: geysering in vertical cryogenic lines, the need to chill down pumps and lines before start, vortexing at tank outlets (cured by anti-vortex baffles), slosh (damped by baffles, Chapter 32) and <em>water hammer</em>. Suddenly stopping a liquid column raises its pressure by <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta p = \\rho a\\Delta u</span> (Joukowsky); for liquid oxygen (<span class=\"mi\">a \\approx 900</span> m/s) stopped from 10 m/s, that is about 10 MPa.</p>"}},"28":{"intro":"","secs":{"28.1":"<p>A solid motor consists of a case (steel, titanium or filament-wound composite), internal insulation and liner, a cast propellant grain, a nozzle (graphite or carbon–carbon throat with an ablative exit cone) and an igniter. Strengths: storable, mechanically simple, high thrust and high density. Weaknesses: lower Isp than liquids, generally no shutdown or throttling once ignited, sensitivity to grain defects (cracks, voids, debonds), and explosive-hazard handling requirements.</p>","28.2":"<p><em>Double-base</em> propellants are homogeneous gels of energetic polymers. <em>Composite</em> propellants are a heterogeneous mixture of crystalline oxidizer particles (commonly ammonium perchlorate) in a rubbery polymer binder, often with powdered aluminum as additional fuel. Composites have densities of about 1.75–1.85 g/cm³ and vacuum Isp of about 265–295 s in large motors. Their mechanical behavior is viscoelastic, sensitive to temperature and to aging. Aluminum combustion produces condensed oxide particles, which cause two-phase flow losses (Chapter 26), and perchlorate exhaust contains HCl.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Omitted content (physical hazard, legal and dual-use)</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Solid propellants are energetic materials. They can ignite or detonate from impact, friction, static electricity or heat, and they are the core technology of missile propulsion.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Three reasons apply together. (1) Physical hazard: mixing, casting and machining accidents are a classic cause of fatalities in propellant work. (2) Legal: manufacture, storage and transport of energetic materials are regulated in most countries. (3) Dual-use: propellant constituents, production equipment and production technology are controlled internationally (Missile Technology Control Regime Annex) and nationally (ITAR and EAR in the United States). What this chapter does give (burning-rate laws, internal ballistics, stability, grain geometry, performance) is standard university material and is sufficient to analyze motors.</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Propellant formulations and ingredient proportions; mixing, casting and curing procedures; igniter compositions; motor-manufacturing methods.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> Fly certified commercial motors through NAR or TRA. Certified adult TRA members may join TRA’s research program, which permits experimental motors under its own safety code and applicable law. University energetic-materials and propulsion laboratories; employment and formal training at licensed propellant and motor manufacturers.</p></aside>","28.3":"<p>The grain surface recedes normal to itself at the burning rate, empirically</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\dot{r} = a\\, p_{c}^{\\, n}\\quad\\text{(Saint-Robert/Vieille law)},</span></p><p>with pressure exponent <span class=\"mi\">n</span> typically 0.2–0.5 for composites. The coefficient <span class=\"mi\">a</span> depends on the initial grain temperature through the <em>temperature sensitivity</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{p} = \\left( \\partial ln\\dot{r}/\\partial T_{i} \\right)_{p}</span>, typically 0.1–0.3% per K. High gas speeds along the port surface increase the rate (<em>erosive burning</em>), important near the aft end of long ports at ignition.</p>","28.4":"<p>Mass conservation in the chamber: gas generated equals gas stored plus gas discharged through the choked nozzle,</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\rho_{p}\\dot{r}A_{b} = \\frac{d\\left( \\rho_{g}V_{c} \\right)}{dt} + \\frac{p_{c}A_{t}}{c^{*}}.</span></p><p>At quasi-steady state, with <span class=\"mi\">K_{n} \\equiv A_{b}/A_{t}</span>:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Equilibrium chamber pressure:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">p_{c} = \\left( \\rho_{p}\\, a\\, c^{*}K_{n} \\right)^{1/(1 - n)}</span>. <strong>Stability:</strong> if <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span> rises slightly, generation (<span class=\"mi\">\\propto p_{c}^{n}</span>) grows less than discharge (<span class=\"mi\">\\propto p_{c}</span>) only if <span class=\"mi\">n &lt; 1</span>. Hence <span class=\"mi\">n &lt; 1</span> is required for stable operation. <strong>Sensitivity:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{\\delta p_{c}}{p_{c}} = \\frac{1}{1 - n}\\frac{\\delta K_{n}}{K_{n}}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{\\delta p_{c}}{p_{c}} = \\frac{\\sigma_{p}\\,\\delta T_{i}}{1 - n}</span>. Low <span class=\"mi\">n</span> makes motors less sensitive to defects and temperature.</p></div><p>Thrust is <span class=\"mi\">F = C_{F}p_{c}A_{t}</span> (Chapter 26), so the thrust–time curve follows the evolution of <span class=\"mi\">A_{b}</span> and of the throat area (which grows by erosion and lowers <span class=\"mi\">p_{c}</span>).</p>","28.5":"<p>The burning surface moves inward in parallel layers, so <span class=\"mi\">A_{b}</span> is a function of the web distance burned. An internally burning cylinder is <em>progressive</em> (rising <span class=\"mi\">A_{b}</span>); an externally burning rod is <em>regressive</em>; star, wagon-wheel and slotted shapes can be made <em>neutral</em>. An end-burner gives long, low-thrust burns. Design measures are the web fraction, the volumetric loading (propellant volume over case volume) and the <em>sliver</em> (propellant left unburned at the end). The grain must also survive structurally: thermal shrinkage after cure, pressurization strain, acceleration and storage aging. Launch boosters shape the thrust curve deliberately, reducing thrust through the max-Q region (Chapter 31).</p>","28.6":"<p>Steering uses a movable nozzle on a flexible bearing (laminated elastomer and metal shims), secondary fluid injection into the nozzle, or jet tabs and vanes. Ablative and graphite materials in the throat must limit erosion, since throat growth lowers pressure and thrust over the burn.</p>","28.7":"<p>A hybrid burns a liquid or gaseous oxidizer over a solid fuel grain. The flame is a diffusion flame standing in the boundary layer above the fuel, and heat transferred to the surface vaporizes the fuel. Regression follows <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{r} = aG_{ox}^{n}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">G_{ox} = {\\dot{m}}_{ox}/A_{port}</span> (classical theory: <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{r} \\propto G^{0.8}x^{- 0.2}</span>, reduced by the “blocking” effect of fuel vapor).</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Mixture-ratio shift.</strong> For one circular port of radius <span class=\"mi\">R</span> and length <span class=\"mi\">L</span>, <span class=\"mi\">{\\dot{m}}_{f} = \\rho_{f}\\dot{r}(2\\pi RL) \\propto R^{1 - 2n}</span> at constant oxidizer flow. For <span class=\"mi\">n &gt; 1/2</span>, fuel flow falls as the port opens and <span class=\"mi\">O/F</span> rises during the burn; at <span class=\"mi\">n = 1/2</span> it stays constant.</p></div><p>Hybrids offer safety (the fuel is inert), throttling, restart and low cost. Their weaknesses are low regression rates (needing long grains or many ports), mixture-ratio shift, incomplete combustion and slivers. Liquefying fuels such as paraffin form a melt layer whose instability entrains droplets, raising regression rates several-fold; this remains an active research area.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Nitrous oxide, a common hybrid oxidizer, can decompose explosively when heated, contaminated or exposed to an ignition source in tanks and lines. In 2007 a nitrous oxide tank explosion during a cold-flow test in Mojave, California, killed three workers. Concentrated hydrogen peroxide decomposes violently on contact with contaminants.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Hybrids are “safer than solids” because their fuel grain is inert (§28.7); their oxidizer systems remain hazardous.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> Student hybrid programs with documented design and safety reviews; oxidizer-handling guidance from industrial gas suppliers and the Compressed Gas Association; faculty-supervised test facilities.</p></aside>","28.8":"<p>Hydrazine decomposes over an iridium-on-alumina catalyst: <span class=\"mi\">3N_{2}H_{4} \\rightarrow 4NH_{3} + N_{2}</span> (exothermic), followed by partial endothermic dissociation of ammonia, <span class=\"mi\">4NH_{3} \\rightarrow 2N_{2} + 6H_{2}</span>. More ammonia dissociation lowers both temperature and molar mass; catalyst-bed design sets the balance. Vacuum Isp is about 220–235 s. <em>Green monopropellants</em> based on hydroxylammonium nitrate (such as AF-M315E/ASCENT, flown in 2019) or ammonium dinitramide (such as LMP-103S, flown in 2010) offer roughly 10% more Isp, higher density and much lower toxicity, but need hotter catalyst beds. High-test hydrogen peroxide gives about 160–180 s.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Physical hazard</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Hydrazine is toxic by inhalation and skin contact and is a suspected carcinogen. Concentrated hydrogen peroxide is a strong oxidizer. Catalyst beds and exhaust are hot and toxic. Spacecraft are fueled by specialists in protective suits.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Toxicity is the main reason for the shift to the “green” monopropellants described in this section.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> Professional propellant-handling training at launch sites and spacecraft manufacturers; NIOSH and OSHA exposure data; NASA and ESA public literature on green propulsion.</p></aside>","28.9":"<p>Pressure-fed, radiation-cooled NTO/MMH thrusters from newtons to kilonewtons deliver 300–325 s and can pulse in milliseconds. The <em>minimum impulse bit</em> sets attitude-control precision. <em>Cold-gas</em> thrusters expand stored gas without combustion. For complete expansion, <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp} = \\sqrt{2\\gamma RT_{0}/(\\gamma - 1)}/g_{0}</span>, about 80 s for nitrogen at 300 K (65–73 s in practice). They are simple, clean and precise but low-performing.</p>","28.10":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>System</th> <th>Vacuum <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{I}_{\\mathbf{sp}}</span> (s)</th> <th>Throttle and restart</th> <th>Storability</th> <th>Main strengths</th> <th>Main limits</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Cryogenic bipropellant</td> <td>330–465</td> <td>Yes</td> <td>Weeks (boil-off)</td> <td>Highest chemical Isp</td> <td>Complexity, cryogenics</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Storable bipropellant</td> <td>300–340</td> <td>Yes</td> <td>Years</td> <td>Reliable restart</td> <td>Toxicity, lower Isp</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Solid</td> <td>250–295</td> <td>Generally no</td> <td>Years</td> <td>Thrust, density, simplicity</td> <td>Isp, no shutdown, hazard class</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Hybrid</td> <td>250–320</td> <td>Yes</td> <td>Long</td> <td>Safety, throttling</td> <td>Regression rate, O/F shift</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Monopropellant</td> <td>160–255</td> <td>Pulsed</td> <td>Years</td> <td>Simplicity</td> <td>Low Isp</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Cold gas</td> <td>50–75</td> <td>Pulsed</td> <td>Years</td> <td>Cleanliness, precision</td> <td>Very low Isp</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div>"}},"29":{"intro":"","secs":{"29.1":"<p>For two point (or spherically symmetric) masses with no other forces, the relative position <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{r}</span> of body 2 with respect to body 1 obeys</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\ddot{\\mathbf{r}} = - \\frac{\\mu}{r^{3}}\\mathbf{r},\\quad\\quad\\mu = G\\left( m_{1} + m_{2} \\right) \\approx Gm_{1}\\ \\ \\left( m_{2} \\ll m_{1} \\right).</span></p>","29.2":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Angular momentum</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{h} = \\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{v}</span> is constant, so the motion is planar and sweeps area at the rate <span class=\"mi\">h/2</span> (Kepler’s second law). <strong>Energy</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon = \\frac{v^{2}}{2} - \\frac{\\mu}{r}</span> is constant. <strong>Eccentricity vector</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{e} = \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{h}}{\\mu} - \\frac{\\mathbf{r}}{r}</span> is constant, points to periapsis, and has magnitude <span class=\"mi\">e</span>.</p></div>","29.3":"<p>Dotting <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{e}</span> with <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{r}</span> gives <span class=\"mi\">re\\cos\\nu = h^{2}/\\mu - r</span>, i.e.</p><p><span class=\"md\">r = \\frac{h^{2}/\\mu}{1 + e\\cos\\nu},\\quad\\quad p = \\frac{h^{2}}{\\mu},</span></p><p>a conic with the attracting body at a focus (Kepler’s first law; geometry in Chapter 4), where <span class=\"mi\">\\nu</span> is the <em>true anomaly</em>. For closed orbits <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon = - \\mu/(2a)</span>, giving the energy equation:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Vis-viva:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">v^{2} = \\mu\\left( \\frac{2}{r} - \\frac{1}{a} \\right)</span>. Circular: <span class=\"mi\">v_{c} = \\sqrt{\\mu/r}</span>. Parabolic (escape): <span class=\"mi\">v = \\sqrt{2\\mu/r}</span>. Hyperbolic: <span class=\"mi\">a &lt; 0</span> and <span class=\"mi\">v_{\\infty}^{2} = - \\mu/a</span>.</p></div>","29.4":"<p>Kepler’s third law: <span class=\"mi\">T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{a^{3}/\\mu}</span>, with mean motion <span class=\"mi\">n = \\sqrt{\\mu/a^{3}}</span>. Velocity components: radial <span class=\"mi\">v_{r} = (\\mu/h)e\\sin\\nu</span> and transverse <span class=\"mi\">v_{\\theta} = (\\mu/h)\\left( 1 + e\\cos\\nu \\right)</span>. The flight-path angle (above the local horizontal) satisfies <span class=\"mi\">\\tan\\gamma = e\\sin\\nu/\\left( 1 + e\\cos\\nu \\right)</span>, and is zero at periapsis and apoapsis.</p>","29.5":"<p>Introduce the <em>eccentric anomaly</em> <span class=\"mi\">E</span> (Chapter 4): <span class=\"mi\">r = a\\left( 1 - e\\cos E \\right)</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\tan(\\nu/2) = \\sqrt{(1 + e)/(1 - e)}\\tan(E/2)</span>. Then:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Kepler’s equation:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">M = E - e\\sin E = n\\left( t - t_{p} \\right)</span>, with mean anomaly <span class=\"mi\">M</span> and periapsis time <span class=\"mi\">t_{p}</span>. Finding the time from position is explicit. Finding the position at a given time requires solving for <span class=\"mi\">E</span> (Newton’s method from <span class=\"mi\">E_{0} = M</span>, Chapter 14). For hyperbolas, <span class=\"mi\">M_{h} = e\\sinh F - F</span>.</p></div><p>Universal-variable formulations handle every conic with one set of equations, which is valuable in software.</p>","29.6":"<p>The <em>Earth-centered inertial</em> (ECI) frame has <span class=\"mi\">z</span> along the rotation axis and <span class=\"mi\">x</span> toward the vernal equinox (J2000 epoch). The <em>perifocal</em> frame (PQW) has <span class=\"mi\">P</span> toward periapsis and <span class=\"mi\">W</span> along <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{h}</span>. The six classical elements are: semi-major axis <span class=\"mi\">a</span>, eccentricity <span class=\"mi\">e</span>, inclination <span class=\"mi\">i</span>, right ascension of the ascending node <span class=\"mi\">\\Omega</span>, argument of periapsis <span class=\"mi\">\\omega</span>, and true anomaly <span class=\"mi\">\\nu</span> (or <span class=\"mi\">M</span> at an epoch). From a state <span class=\"mi\">\\left( \\mathbf{r},\\mathbf{v} \\right)</span>: compute <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{h}</span>, the node vector <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{n} = \\widehat{\\mathbf{z}} \\times \\mathbf{h}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{e}</span>. Then <span class=\"mi\">i = arccos\\left( h_{z}/h \\right)</span>; <span class=\"mi\">\\Omega</span> is the angle from <span class=\"mi\">\\widehat{\\mathbf{x}}</span> to <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{n}</span>; <span class=\"mi\">\\omega</span> is the angle from <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{n}</span> to <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{e}</span>; <span class=\"mi\">\\nu</span> is the angle from <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{e}</span> to <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{r}</span>. Resolve each quadrant with signs: <span class=\"mi\">n_{y}</span> for <span class=\"mi\">\\Omega</span>, <span class=\"mi\">e_{z}</span> for <span class=\"mi\">\\omega</span>, and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}</span> for <span class=\"mi\">\\nu</span>. The rotation from perifocal to ECI is <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{R}_{3}( - \\Omega)\\mathbf{R}_{1}( - i)\\mathbf{R}_{3}( - \\omega)</span>. For circular or equatorial orbits <span class=\"mi\">\\omega</span> or <span class=\"mi\">\\Omega</span> is undefined; use the argument of latitude <span class=\"mi\">u = \\omega + \\nu</span>, the true longitude, or equinoctial elements.</p>","29.7":"<p>A burn short compared with the orbital period changes velocity instantaneously by <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta\\mathbf{v}</span>; propellant follows from the rocket equation.</p><ul> <li><strong>Hohmann transfer</strong> between coplanar circular orbits <span class=\"mi\">r_{1} &lt; r_{2}</span>: an ellipse with <span class=\"mi\">a_{t} = \\left( r_{1} + r_{2} \\right)/2</span>, tangent to both. <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta v_{1} = \\sqrt{\\mu\\left( 2/r_{1} - 1/a_{t} \\right)} - \\sqrt{\\mu/r_{1}}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta v_{2} = \\sqrt{\\mu/r_{2}} - \\sqrt{\\mu\\left( 2/r_{2} - 1/a_{t} \\right)}</span>, and the transfer time is <span class=\"mi\">\\pi\\sqrt{a_{t}^{3}/\\mu}</span>. It is the optimal two-impulse transfer.</li> <li><strong>Bi-elliptic transfer:</strong> three impulses through an intermediate apoapsis. It beats Hohmann for <span class=\"mi\">r_{2}/r_{1} &gt; 15.58</span> (for any intermediate radius); between 11.94 and 15.58 it depends on that radius; and it is slower.</li> <li><strong>Plane change</strong> at speed <span class=\"mi\">v</span>: <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta v = 2v\\sin(\\Delta i/2)</span>. Do it where <span class=\"mi\">v</span> is smallest (apoapsis, high altitude), and combine it with other burns: rotating one vector into another costs <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta v = \\sqrt{v_{1}^{2} + v_{2}^{2} - 2v_{1}v_{2}\\cos\\Delta i}</span> (law of cosines, Chapter 3).</li> <li><strong>Phasing:</strong> a temporary orbit of different period lets a spacecraft catch up with, or wait for, a target in the same orbit.</li> </ul><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Oberth effect.</strong> The energy change from a small burn is <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta\\varepsilon \\approx v\\,\\Delta v</span>. Burns deep in a gravity well, at high speed, change orbital energy most per unit Δv. This is why departure burns are made at periapsis of a low parking orbit.</p></div>","29.8":"<p>The hyperbolic excess speed <span class=\"mi\">v_{\\infty}</span> satisfies <span class=\"mi\">v_{\\infty}^{2} = v^{2} - 2\\mu/r</span>, and the characteristic energy is <span class=\"mi\">C_{3} = v_{\\infty}^{2}</span>. Departing from a circular parking orbit of radius <span class=\"mi\">r</span> costs <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta v = \\sqrt{v_{\\infty}^{2} + 2\\mu/r} - \\sqrt{\\mu/r}</span>. The hyperbola bends the trajectory through <span class=\"mi\">\\delta = 2\\arcsin(1/e)</span> (Chapter 30).</p>","29.9":"<p>Spherical trigonometry (Chapter 3) relates inclination <span class=\"mi\">i</span>, site latitude <span class=\"mi\">\\phi</span> and launch azimuth <span class=\"mi\">A_{z}</span> (measured from north): <span class=\"mi\">\\cos i = cos\\phi\\sin A_{z}</span>. The minimum inclination reachable directly is therefore <span class=\"mi\">|\\phi|</span>, by launching due east. Lower inclinations require a costly plane change. The orbital plane passes over a site twice per day, defining <em>launch windows</em> for a given target plane (Chapter 31 adds Earth rotation). A circular orbit’s ground track shifts west each revolution by about <span class=\"mi\">360^{\\circ} \\times T/(23.93\\ h)</span>.</p>","29.10":"<p>Geostationary orbit: <span class=\"mi\">r = 42\\, 164</span> km, <span class=\"mi\">i = 0</span>, period one sidereal day (86 164 s). Geosynchronous transfer orbit (GTO): perigee near LEO altitude, apogee at GEO radius. Molniya: 12 h period at the critical inclination 63.4°, so its apogee does not drift (Chapter 30). Sun-synchronous orbits (Chapter 30). Medium Earth orbit carries the navigation constellations (about 20 000 km altitude).</p>"}},"30":{"intro":"","secs":{"30.1":"<p><em>Cowell’s method</em> integrates <span class=\"mi\">\\ddot{\\mathbf{r}} = - \\mu\\mathbf{r}/r^{3} + \\mathbf{a}_{p}</span> numerically, which is simple and general. <em>Encke’s method</em> integrates only the deviation from a reference conic. <em>Variation of parameters</em> tracks how the osculating elements drift. With perturbing acceleration components <span class=\"mi\">F_{R}</span> (radial), <span class=\"mi\">F_{S}</span> (along-track) and <span class=\"mi\">F_{W}</span> (normal), and argument of latitude <span class=\"mi\">u = \\omega + \\nu</span>, the Gauss equations include:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\frac{da}{dt} = \\frac{2a^{2}}{h}\\left( e\\sin\\nu\\, F_{R} + \\frac{p}{r}F_{S} \\right),\\quad\\quad\\frac{di}{dt} = \\frac{r\\cos u}{h}F_{W},\\quad\\quad\\frac{d\\Omega}{dt} = \\frac{r\\sin u}{h\\sin i}F_{W}.</span></p><p>They show directly that along-track thrust changes <span class=\"mi\">a</span> and normal thrust changes the plane.</p>","30.2":"<p>The potential is <span class=\"mi\">U = \\frac{\\mu}{r}\\left\\lbrack 1 - \\sum_{n \\geq 2}^{}J_{n}\\left( \\frac{R}{r} \\right)^{n}P_{n}\\left( \\sin\\phi \\right) + \\text{tesseral terms} \\right\\rbrack</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">J_{2} = 1.08263 \\times 10^{- 3}</span> for the equatorial bulge. Averaged over an orbit, J₂ produces secular drifts:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\Omega} = - \\frac{3}{2}nJ_{2}\\left( \\frac{R}{p} \\right)^{2}\\cos i</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\quad\\dot{\\omega} = \\frac{3}{4}nJ_{2}\\left( \\frac{R}{p} \\right)^{2}\\left( 5\\cos^{2}i - 1 \\right)</span>. The node regresses for prograde orbits. Apsides freeze at the <strong>critical inclination</strong> 63.43° (Molniya orbits). <strong>Sun-synchronous:</strong> choose <span class=\"mi\">i</span> so that <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\Omega} = + {0.9856}^{\\circ}</span>/day, which requires a slightly retrograde inclination (96–100° in LEO). The orbit plane then keeps a fixed orientation to the Sun.</p></div>","30.3":"<p><span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{a}_{D} = - \\frac{1}{2\\beta}\\rho v_{rel}^{2}{\\widehat{\\mathbf{v}}}_{rel}</span>, with ballistic coefficient <span class=\"mi\">\\beta = m/\\left( C_{D}A \\right)</span> (free-molecular <span class=\"mi\">C_{D} \\approx 2.0</span>–2.2, Chapter 40). For a near-circular orbit, energy loss gives <span class=\"mi\">da/dt = - \\rho\\sqrt{\\mu a}/\\beta</span>. Drag lowers the orbit and, paradoxically, raises the speed. Thermospheric density varies by an order of magnitude with solar activity (F10.7 index) and geomagnetic storms. Reentry-time predictions therefore carry uncertainties of order ±10–20% of the remaining lifetime.</p>","30.4":"<p>The perturbation from a third body is the <em>difference</em> between its pull on the satellite and on the central body. Luni-solar perturbations drive GEO inclination growth of roughly 0.75–0.95° per year, which station-keeping must remove. Solar radiation pressure gives <span class=\"mi\">a = P_{\\odot}C_{R}A/m</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">P_{\\odot} = 4.56 \\times 10^{- 6}</span> N/m² at 1 AU and reflectivity coefficient <span class=\"mi\">C_{R}</span> between 1 and 2. It dominates for high area-to-mass objects and requires eclipse modeling.</p>","30.5":"<p>Given two position vectors and a time of flight, find the connecting conic (and <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{v}_{1}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{v}_{2}</span>). <em>Lambert’s theorem</em>: the transfer time depends only on <span class=\"mi\">a</span>, <span class=\"mi\">r_{1} + r_{2}</span> and the chord <span class=\"mi\">c</span>. For zero revolutions and a specified direction the solution is unique; multi-revolution solutions come in pairs. Robust solvers (universal variables, Gooding, Izzo) iterate on one variable. Repeating Lambert solutions over grids of departure and arrival dates produces <em>porkchop plots</em> of <span class=\"mi\">C_{3}</span> and arrival <span class=\"mi\">v_{\\infty}</span>, the starting point of mission design.</p>","30.6":"<p>Inside a planet’s <em>sphere of influence</em>, <span class=\"mi\">r_{SOI} = a_{p}\\left( m_{p}/M_{\\odot} \\right)^{2/5}</span> (about 925 000 km for Earth), treat the motion as planet-centered; outside, as Sun-centered. Patch the two at the boundary, where the planet-relative <span class=\"mi\">v_{\\infty}</span> connects them. The Earth–Mars Hohmann transfer requires <span class=\"mi\">v_{\\infty} \\approx 2.94</span> km/s at departure and 2.65 km/s at arrival and takes about 259 days. Opportunities recur every <em>synodic period</em> <span class=\"mi\">1/\\left| 1/T_{1} - 1/T_{2} \\right|</span>, about 780 days for Mars. Arrival may use propulsive capture, aerocapture or aerobraking.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Gravity assist.</strong> In the planet’s frame a flyby only rotates <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{v}_{\\infty}</span>, by <span class=\"mi\">\\delta = 2\\arcsin\\left\\lbrack 1/\\left( 1 + r_{p}v_{\\infty}^{2}/\\mu_{p} \\right) \\right\\rbrack</span>, without changing its magnitude. In the Sun’s frame, adding the planet’s velocity converts that rotation into a heliocentric speed change of up to <span class=\"mi\">2v_{\\infty}\\sin(\\delta/2)</span>, at no propellant cost. Momentum is borrowed from the planet’s orbit.</p></div>","30.7":"<p>In a frame rotating with a target in a circular orbit of mean motion <span class=\"mi\">n</span> (<span class=\"mi\">x</span> radial outward, <span class=\"mi\">y</span> along-track, <span class=\"mi\">z</span> cross-track), the linearized relative motion obeys the <strong>Clohessy–Wiltshire (Hill) equations</strong>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\ddot{x} - 3n^{2}x - 2n\\dot{y} = a_{x},\\quad\\quad\\ddot{y} + 2n\\dot{x} = a_{y},\\quad\\quad\\ddot{z} + n^{2}z = a_{z}.</span></p><p>Consequences that defy intuition: a chaser below the target drifts <em>ahead</em> by <span class=\"mi\">3\\pi|x|</span> per orbit; thrusting forward raises the orbit and makes the chaser fall behind. Cross-track motion is a simple oscillation. Rendezvous planning uses the closed-form solutions to compute two-impulse transfers, approaches along the velocity vector (V-bar) or radius vector (R-bar), and passively safe relative orbits.</p>","30.8":"<p>Two primaries in circular orbit about their barycenter, plus a massless third body, viewed in the rotating frame with mass parameter <span class=\"mi\">\\mu = m_{2}/\\left( m_{1} + m_{2} \\right)</span>. The <em>Jacobi constant</em> <span class=\"mi\">C = 2U^{*} - v^{2}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">U^{*} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( x^{2} + y^{2} \\right) + (1 - \\mu)/r_{1} + \\mu/r_{2}</span>, is conserved. Surfaces of zero velocity bound the accessible regions. There are five equilibria: the collinear points L1–L3 (unstable, but with families of periodic halo and Lyapunov orbits) and the triangular points L4 and L5 (stable for <span class=\"mi\">\\mu &lt; 0.0385</span>). The stable and unstable <em>invariant manifolds</em> of periodic orbits form low-energy transfer pathways, used by Genesis and by ballistic lunar transfers. Near-rectilinear halo orbits about the Moon support lunar-orbit operations.</p>","30.9":"<p>Measurements (range, range-rate, angles, GNSS pseudoranges) are related to the state through models. <em>Initial orbit determination</em> uses three position vectors (Gibbs) or three angle sets (Gauss, Laplace). <em>Batch least squares</em> (differential correction) linearizes the residuals about a reference trajectory using the state-transition matrix and solves the normal equations (Chapters 10 and 13). <em>Sequential</em> estimation uses an extended Kalman filter (Chapter 46). Realistic covariance underlies <em>conjunction assessment</em>, the computation of collision probability between space objects.</p>","30.10":"<p>With continuous small acceleration the orbit spirals. For a circle-to-circle transfer with plane change <span class=\"mi\">\\Delta i</span> (rad) at constant acceleration with optimal steering, Edelbaum’s approximation gives</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\Delta v = \\sqrt{v_{0}^{2} - 2v_{0}v_{1}\\cos\\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\Delta i \\right) + v_{1}^{2}}.</span></p><p>Low-thrust Δv exceeds impulsive Δv because thrust is spent away from the most efficient points, but the high <span class=\"mi\">I_{sp}</span> of electric propulsion (Chapter 48) more than compensates.</p>"}},"31":{"intro":"","secs":{"31.1":"<p>For a point mass in a vertical plane over a spherical, non-rotating Earth, let <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span> be the flight-path angle above the local horizontal and <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span> the angle of attack (thrust along the body axis):</p><div class=\"key\"><p><span class=\"mi\">\\dot{V} = \\frac{T\\cos\\alpha - D}{m} - g\\sin\\gamma</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad V\\dot{\\gamma} = \\frac{T\\sin\\alpha + L}{m} - \\left( g - \\frac{V^{2}}{r} \\right)\\cos\\gamma</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\dot{h} = V\\sin\\gamma</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\dot{x} = V\\cos\\gamma\\frac{R_{\\oplus}}{r}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\quad\\dot{m} = - {\\dot{m}}_{p}</span>, with <span class=\"mi\">g = \\mu/r^{2}</span>.</p></div><p>The term <span class=\"mi\">V^{2}/r</span> is the centrifugal relief that eventually makes horizontal flight self-sustaining (orbit). Earth rotation adds Coriolis and centripetal terms. Engineering simulations are three-degree-of-freedom (point mass, attitude assumed) or six-degree-of-freedom (with rotational dynamics and control, Chapter 32).</p>","31.2":"<p>Use the standard atmosphere (Appendix D) for design and measured or statistical profiles for operations. Upper-level winds and wind shear dominate aerodynamic loads. On launch day, balloon or radar wind measurements feed load assessments, and launch-commit criteria can stop a countdown.</p>","31.3":"<ol type=\"1\"> <li><strong>Vertical rise</strong> to clear the tower.</li> <li><strong>Pitch-over:</strong> a small “kick” angle at low speed starts the turn.</li> <li><strong>Gravity turn:</strong> fly at <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha \\approx 0</span> so that thrust follows the velocity. Gravity then rotates the velocity toward horizontal by itself (<span class=\"mi\">V\\dot{\\gamma} = - \\left( g - V^{2}/r \\right)\\cos\\gamma</span>), and lateral aerodynamic loads stay small.</li> <li><strong>Max-Q:</strong> many vehicles throttle down (a “bucket”) to cap <span class=\"mi\">q</span> near 30–35 kPa.</li> <li><strong>Staging</strong> at high altitude and low <span class=\"mi\">q</span>; the fairing is jettisoned once free-molecular heating falls below a threshold (a common criterion is about 1.1 kW/m²).</li> <li><strong>Guided upper-stage steering</strong> in vacuum: for a flat Earth with no atmosphere, the minimum-time steering law is the <em>linear tangent law</em> <span class=\"mi\">\\tan\\theta = a - bt</span> (Chapter 46). Closed-loop guidance solves for its parameters in flight.</li> <li><strong>Insertion</strong>, either directly or via a coast to apogee followed by a circularization burn.</li> </ol>","31.4":"<p>Rearranging the <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{V}</span> equation and integrating over the burn:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\Delta v_{ideal} = c\\ln\\frac{m_{0}}{m_{f}} = \\int\\frac{T}{m}dt = \\Delta V + \\int g\\sin\\gamma\\, dt + \\int\\frac{D}{m}dt + \\int\\frac{T}{m}\\left( 1 - \\cos\\alpha \\right)\\, dt.</span></p><p>The terms on the right are the velocity gain, the gravity loss, the drag loss and the steering loss. Typical values to LEO: gravity 1.0–1.5 km/s, drag 0.1–0.3 km/s, steering 0.05–0.2 km/s. Higher thrust-to-weight reduces gravity loss but raises drag loss (higher <span class=\"mi\">q</span> low down) and engine mass. First-stage liftoff <span class=\"mi\">T/W</span> is typically 1.2–1.5, and upper stages often run at <span class=\"mi\">T/W</span> below 1, since by then <span class=\"mi\">\\gamma</span> is small and gravity loss accrues slowly.</p>","31.5":"<p>Bending loads scale with the normal force, which is proportional to <span class=\"mi\">q\\alpha</span> (Chapter 25). A crosswind <span class=\"mi\">w</span> produces <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha \\approx w/V</span>, so wind shear near max-Q is the critical case. <em>Load relief</em> steers partially into the wind, using accelerometer or angle-of-attack feedback to reduce <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha</span> at the cost of trajectory dispersion. Every vehicle carries a <span class=\"mi\">q\\alpha</span> (and <span class=\"mi\">q\\beta</span>) limit derived from its structural allowables (Chapter 33).</p>","31.6":"<p>Trajectory design maximizes payload (or minimizes propellant) subject to: maximum <span class=\"mi\">q</span>; aerodynamic heating limits; acceleration (about 4–6g for crews and sensitive payloads); stage drop zones; ground-track and range-safety corridors; and terminal insertion conditions. Early designs parameterize the pitch program and optimize the parameters. Rigorous designs solve an optimal-control problem (Chapter 46).</p>","31.7":"<p>Thrust, Isp, mass, aerodynamic coefficients, winds and density are uncertain. Monte Carlo simulation (Chapter 13) gives the distribution of insertion errors and propellant consumption. A <em>flight performance reserve</em> of propellant is carried so that the 3σ (or 99.7%) case still reaches orbit.</p>","31.8":"<p>Propulsive return of a first stage requires a <em>boostback</em> burn to reverse horizontal velocity (for a return to the launch site), an <em>entry burn</em> to reduce velocity and heating through the dense atmosphere, aerodynamic steering (for example grid fins), and a <em>landing burn</em>. If the minimum-throttle thrust exceeds the stage’s empty weight, it cannot hover, so ignition must be timed so that velocity reaches zero exactly at touchdown. The propellant reserved for recovery reduces payload, more for return to the launch site than for downrange landing.</p>","31.9":"<p>During flight, the <em>instantaneous impact point</em> (where the vehicle would land if thrust stopped now) is tracked continuously. A flight termination system, increasingly autonomous, ends thrust if the vehicle leaves its safety corridor. In the United States, FAA launch licensing (14 CFR Part 450) caps public risk. A typical criterion is an expected casualty of at most <span class=\"mi\">10^{- 4}</span> per launch. Launch sites are chosen for over-water downrange corridors and favorable latitudes.</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Legal and dual-use (scope note)</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> Flight termination systems use pyrotechnic or explosive devices, and launch and reentry operations require licenses.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Ordnance is regulated explosive technology, and destruct-system design is safety-critical work done only under the authority of a launch range or licensing agency.</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Flight-termination ordnance and destruct-system design.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> The Range Commanders Council’s public flight-termination-system standard (RCC 319); FAA Part 450 guidance documents and advisory circulars; range-safety training through launch-site operators and government ranges; professional employment in range safety.</p></aside>"}},"32":{"intro":"","secs":{"32.1":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Representation</th> <th>Parameters</th> <th>Singularities</th> <th>Notes</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Direction-cosine matrix <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{C}</span></td> <td>9 (6 constraints)</td> <td>None</td> <td>Composes by multiplication</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Euler angles (e.g. 3-2-1: yaw ψ, pitch θ, roll φ)</td> <td>3</td> <td>At θ = ±90° (“gimbal lock”)</td> <td>Intuitive; 12 possible sequences</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Euler axis and angle <span class=\"mi\">\\left( \\widehat{\\mathbf{e}},\\Phi \\right)</span></td> <td>4</td> <td>Axis undefined at Φ = 0</td> <td>Euler’s theorem: every rotation is about one axis</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Quaternion <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{q} = \\left\\lbrack \\cos\\frac{\\Phi}{2},\\ \\widehat{\\mathbf{e}}\\sin\\frac{\\Phi}{2} \\right\\rbrack</span></td> <td>4 (unit norm)</td> <td>None (but <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{q}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">- \\mathbf{q}</span> are the same attitude)</td> <td>Standard in flight software</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Modified Rodrigues parameters <span class=\"mi\">\\widehat{\\mathbf{e}}\\tan\\frac{\\Phi}{4}</span></td> <td>3</td> <td>At Φ = 360° (switch to the shadow set)</td> <td>Minimal; good for estimation</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>The DCM from Euler axis and angle is <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{C} = cos\\Phi\\,\\mathbf{I} + \\left( 1 - \\cos\\Phi \\right)\\widehat{\\mathbf{e}}{\\widehat{\\mathbf{e}}}^{T} - \\sin\\Phi\\left\\lbrack \\widehat{\\mathbf{e}} \\times \\right\\rbrack</span>. <em>Kinematics:</em> for the DCM mapping inertial to body components with body rates <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\omega}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\mathbf{C}} = - \\left\\lbrack \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\right\\rbrack\\mathbf{C}</span>. For quaternions (scalar first, Hamilton product), <span class=\"mi\">\\dot{\\mathbf{q}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{q} \\otimes \\left\\lbrack 0,\\ \\mathbf{\\omega} \\right\\rbrack</span>. For 3-2-1 Euler angles with body rates <span class=\"mi\">(p,q,r)</span>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\dot{\\phi} = p + \\left( q\\sin\\phi + r\\cos\\phi \\right)\\tan\\theta,\\quad\\quad\\dot{\\theta} = q\\cos\\phi - r\\sin\\phi,\\quad\\quad\\dot{\\psi} = \\frac{q\\sin\\phi + r\\cos\\phi}{\\cos\\theta},</span></p><p>which blow up at <span class=\"mi\">\\theta = \\pm 90^{\\circ}</span>.</p><div class=\"pit\"><p><strong>Conventions.</strong> Quaternion conventions differ (scalar first or last; Hamilton or JPL/Shuster product order), and DCMs may be active or passive. Interface errors between conventions have caused real mission anomalies. State the convention in every document and code module, and test with known rotations.</p></div>","32.2":"<p>Euler’s equations (Chapter 15) govern rotation. <em>Spin stabilization</em> exploits gyroscopic stiffness. With internal energy dissipation (flexing antennas, fuel slosh), only spin about the <em>major</em> axis is stable. Explorer 1 (1958), an elongated body spun about its long (minor) axis, went into a flat spin within hours. <em>Dual-spin</em> spacecraft carry a despun platform; their stability depends on where dissipation occurs. Nutation dampers remove residual coning.</p>","32.3":"<ul> <li><strong>Gravity gradient:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{T}_{gg} = \\frac{3\\mu}{r^{3}}\\widehat{\\mathbf{r}} \\times \\left( \\mathbf{I}\\widehat{\\mathbf{r}} \\right)</span> in body components. Its magnitude in one axis is <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{3\\mu}{2r^{3}}\\left| I_{z} - I_{y} \\right|\\sin 2\\theta</span>. It can also be used for passive stabilization.</li> <li><strong>Aerodynamic:</strong> drag acting at a center of pressure offset from the center of mass; dominant below about 500 km.</li> <li><strong>Solar radiation pressure:</strong> also acting through a CP–CM offset; dominant at high altitude.</li> <li><strong>Magnetic:</strong> residual dipole <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{m}</span> crossed with the geomagnetic field <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{B}</span>.</li> <li><strong>Internal:</strong> moving parts, propellant slosh and thruster misalignment.</li> </ul>","32.4":"<p><em>Thrusters</em> (RCS) give large torques but have a minimum impulse bit and consume propellant. <em>Reaction wheels</em> exchange momentum with the body (body torque <span class=\"mi\">= - {\\dot{\\mathbf{h}}}_{w}</span>), are smooth and precise, and saturate under secular disturbances; they are then <em>desaturated</em> with thrusters or magnetorquers. <em>Control moment gyros</em> tilt constant-speed rotors and produce large torques, <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\tau} = \\dot{\\mathbf{\\delta}} \\times \\mathbf{h}</span>, but have geometric singularities that steering laws must avoid. <em>Magnetorquers</em> produce <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{m} \\times \\mathbf{B}</span>, so they cannot torque about <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{B}</span> at any instant. Launch vehicles use <em>gimballed engines</em> (hydraulic or electromechanical actuators), aerodynamic fins or grid fins, differential throttling, and roll thrusters.</p>","32.5":"<p>Gyroscopes (ring-laser, fiber-optic, hemispherical-resonator, MEMS) measure rates and are integrated between absolute fixes. Star trackers give arcsecond-class absolute attitude at a few hertz. Sun sensors range from coarse to fine. Magnetometers give degree-level attitude in LEO. Horizon sensors measure Earth’s limb. Multi-antenna GNSS can measure attitude directly. Error models are covered in Chapter 34.</p>","32.6":"<p><strong>TRIAD</strong> builds two orthonormal triads from two measured vectors (for example Sun and magnetic field) and their reference directions. <strong>Wahba’s problem</strong> finds the rotation <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{A}</span> minimizing <span class=\"mi\">\\sum w_{i}\\left| \\mathbf{b}_{i} - \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{r}_{i} \\right|^{2}</span> and is solved optimally by Davenport’s q-method (an eigenvalue problem), QUEST or the SVD (Chapter 10). In flight, gyro-propagated attitude is corrected by vector measurements in a <em>multiplicative extended Kalman filter</em> that estimates a small error quaternion and the gyro biases (Chapter 46).</p>","32.7":"<p>A standard spacecraft law is quaternion feedback with gyroscopic compensation:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\mathbf{u} = - K_{p}\\, sign\\left( q_{e0} \\right)\\,\\mathbf{q}_{e,v} - K_{d}\\mathbf{\\omega}_{e} + \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{I\\omega},</span></p><p>where <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{q}_{e}</span> is the error quaternion. The sign term makes the vehicle take the shorter rotation and avoids “unwinding” a full turn. Large slews rotate about the <em>eigenaxis</em> with rate limits. The time-optimal rest-to-rest single-axis slew with torque limit <span class=\"mi\">\\tau_{\\max}</span> is bang-bang, with <span class=\"mi\">t = 2\\sqrt{\\theta/\\alpha}</span> where <span class=\"mi\">\\alpha = \\tau_{\\max}/I</span>. Thruster-only systems use deadbands and switching lines in the phase plane and settle into limit cycles whose amplitude is set by the minimum impulse bit.</p>","32.8":"<p>The design model combines unstable rigid-body aerodynamics (Chapter 22), gimbal-actuator dynamics, flexible bending modes, propellant slosh and sensor dynamics. <em>Bending:</em> long launchers have first bending modes at a few hertz. The local slope of each mode shape at the gyro location determines whether that mode is <em>gain-stabilized</em> (attenuated with notch or low-pass filters) or <em>phase-stabilized</em> (kept in phase so that control adds damping). <em>Slosh:</em> the first lateral mode of liquid in a cylindrical tank of radius <span class=\"mi\">R</span> and fill depth <span class=\"mi\">h</span> has frequency <span class=\"mi\">\\omega^{2} = \\left( 1.841\\, g_{ax}/R \\right)\\tanh(1.841h/R)</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">g_{ax}</span> is the axial acceleration. It is modeled as a pendulum or spring-mass and damped with baffles. Gains are scheduled with dynamic pressure, mass and Mach number. Robustness is verified by stability margins at each flight condition and by Monte Carlo simulation.</p>"}},"33":{"intro":"","secs":{"33.1":"<p><em>Limit load</em> is the largest load expected in service; <em>ultimate load</em> is limit load times the factor of safety (FS), typically 1.25 for uncrewed and 1.4 for crewed structures, with separate yield factors of 1.0–1.25. The <em>margin of safety</em> is</p><p><span class=\"md\">MS = \\frac{\\text{allowable}}{FS \\times \\text{applied}} - 1 \\geq 0.</span></p><p>Structures not verified by test carry higher “no-test” factors. <em>Fracture control</em> ensures that undetected flaws cannot grow to failure within the service life (Chapter 20).</p>","33.2":"<p>Sources: quasi-static accelerations (thrust, maneuvers, typically 4–6g axial and 1–2g lateral at payload interfaces); internal pressure (ullage plus hydrostatic head, Chapter 18); aerodynamic loads (<span class=\"mi\">q\\alpha</span>, Chapter 31); transients (ignition, shutdown, staging); sine and random vibration; acoustics (liftoff and transonic flight); pyrotechnic shock; and thermal loads. For a thin cylinder of radius <span class=\"mi\">R</span> under axial force <span class=\"mi\">P</span> and bending moment <span class=\"mi\">M</span>, the worst compressive line load is <span class=\"mi\">N = P/(2\\pi R) + M/\\left( \\pi R^{2} \\right)</span>. This is the line load of an <em>equivalent axial load</em> <span class=\"mi\">P_{eq} = P + 2M/R</span>. Internal pressure relieves compression and stabilizes thin tanks (pressure stabilization).</p>","33.3":"<p>Options include monocoque shells; semi-monocoque skin–stringer–frame construction; integrally machined isogrid or orthogrid panels; honeycomb sandwich, whose bending stiffness per unit width is about <span class=\"mi\">E_{f}t_{f}h^{2}/2</span> for thin face sheets of thickness <span class=\"mi\">t_{f}</span> separated by <span class=\"mi\">h</span>; and composite overwrapped structures. Thin-walled beams carry shear as shear flow, and closed cells carry torsion efficiently (Chapter 20).</p>","33.4":"<p>Unstiffened cylinders in axial compression buckle far below the classical value <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{cl} = Et/\\left( R\\sqrt{3\\left( 1 - \\nu^{2} \\right)} \\right)</span> because they are highly sensitive to geometric imperfections. The classic design rule (NASA SP-8007) multiplies <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma_{cl}</span> by the empirical knockdown factor</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\gamma = 1 - 0.901\\left( 1 - e^{- \\phi} \\right),\\quad\\quad\\phi = \\frac{1}{16}\\sqrt{\\frac{R}{t}}.</span></p><p>Internal pressure raises the allowable. Stiffened shells must be checked for local skin, panel and general instability modes. Recent work derives less conservative knockdown factors from measured imperfections and high-fidelity nonlinear analysis validated by test.</p>","33.5":"<p>A unidirectional lamina is orthotropic, described by <span class=\"mi\">E_{1}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">E_{2}</span>, <span class=\"mi\">G_{12}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">\\nu_{12}</span>. In plane stress its reduced stiffness matrix <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{Q}</span> is rotated to each ply angle. <em>Classical lamination theory</em> relates resultant forces <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{N}</span> and moments <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{M}</span> to midplane strains <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\varepsilon}^{0}</span> and curvatures <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{\\kappa}</span>:</p><p><span class=\"md\">\\begin{bmatrix} \\mathbf{N} \\\\ \\mathbf{M} \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\mathbf{A} &amp; \\mathbf{B} \\\\ \\mathbf{B} &amp; \\mathbf{D} \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} \\mathbf{\\varepsilon}^{0} \\\\ \\mathbf{\\kappa} \\end{bmatrix}.</span></p><p>Symmetric layups have <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{B} = 0</span> (no stretch–bend coupling); balanced layups avoid extension–shear coupling; <span class=\"mi\">\\lbrack 0/ \\pm 45/90\\rbrack_{s}</span> is quasi-isotropic in-plane. Failure criteria include maximum stress or strain, Tsai–Wu (interactive) and Hashin (separate fiber and matrix modes). Design must also account for impact damage (compression after impact), hot/wet environmental knockdowns, joints and residual thermal stresses. Composite cryogenic tanks must resist matrix microcracking that opens leak paths.</p>","33.6":"<p>A single-degree-of-freedom system has <span class=\"mi\">\\omega_{n} = \\sqrt{k/m}</span> and a resonant amplification <span class=\"mi\">Q = 1/(2\\zeta)</span>. Multi-degree-of-freedom systems obey <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{M}\\ddot{\\mathbf{x}} + \\mathbf{C}\\dot{\\mathbf{x}} + \\mathbf{Kx} = \\mathbf{F}</span>. Their modes come from <span class=\"mi\">\\left( \\mathbf{K} - \\omega^{2}\\mathbf{M} \\right)\\mathbf{\\phi} = 0</span>, and responses are built by modal superposition with 1–3% modal damping typical. <em>Random vibration</em> is specified as an acceleration spectral density (g²/Hz). The RMS acceleration is <span class=\"mi\">\\sqrt{\\int ASD\\, df}</span>, and for a single-degree-of-freedom system:</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Miles’ equation:</strong> <span class=\"mi\">G_{rms} = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{2}f_{n}Q\\, ASD\\left( f_{n} \\right)}</span>. Design to about <span class=\"mi\">3G_{rms}</span> peaks.</p></div><p>Acoustic levels (dB re 20 μPa) excite large panels, analyzed with finite elements at low frequency and statistical energy analysis at high frequency. Pyroshock is specified by a <em>shock response spectrum</em>. <em>Coupled loads analysis</em> combines launcher and payload models through the flight transients. Launch vehicles therefore impose minimum payload frequencies (typically tens of hertz axially and somewhat lower laterally) to avoid dynamic coupling.</p>","33.7":"<p><em>Static aeroelasticity:</em> wing torsional divergence and control reversal. <em>Flutter:</em> a dynamic instability in which aerodynamic forces couple two structural modes (bending and torsion) and extract energy from the flow. <em>Panel flutter</em> of thin skins in supersonic flow is analyzed with <em>piston theory</em>, <span class=\"mi\">p - p_{\\infty} = \\rho_{\\infty}a_{\\infty}\\left( \\partial w/\\partial t + U_{\\infty}\\partial w/\\partial x \\right)</span>. <em>POGO</em> is a longitudinal instability: a structural mode modulates the pump inlet pressure, which modulates propellant flow and thrust, which drives the mode. It caused severe vibration on Saturn V flights (Apollo 6; the Apollo 13 second-stage center engine). The cure is to detune the feed system, typically with gas-charged accumulators in the propellant lines.</p>","33.8":"<p>Structures see thermal stresses and thermal buckling, mismatched expansion at joints, and large cryogenic contraction (aluminum shrinks about 0.4% from room temperature to 20 K). Hot hypersonic structures couple heating, deformation and flow (<em>aerothermoelasticity</em>): deformation changes the flow, which changes heating, which changes stiffness (Chapters 42 and 53).</p>","33.9":"<p>Starting from the principle of virtual work (Chapter 15), interpolate displacements within elements by shape functions. Each element contributes a stiffness matrix; for an axial bar, <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{k}_{e} = (EA/L)\\begin{bmatrix} 1 &amp; - 1 \\\\  - 1 &amp; 1 \\end{bmatrix}</span>. Assemble the global system <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{Ku} = \\mathbf{F}</span>, apply boundary conditions, solve, and recover strains and stresses. Element families include bars, beams, shells and solids. Accuracy improves by mesh refinement (<span class=\"mi\">h</span>) or higher-order elements (<span class=\"mi\">p</span>). Common pitfalls: stress singularities at sharp corners or point loads, poor element shapes, wrong boundary conditions and inconsistent units. Always check results against hand calculations and validate the model by test. Linear buckling eigenvalues overpredict shell strength; use nonlinear analysis with imperfections.</p>","33.10":"<p>Early mass comes from structural-efficiency indices and historical data, with maturity-based growth allowances. The test program comprises development tests; <em>qualification</em> (static tests to ultimate, sometimes to failure; modal survey; vibration, acoustic and shock at qualification levels above flight); <em>acceptance</em> of every flight article at flight-like levels to screen workmanship; and <em>proof testing</em> of pressure vessels, which also screens flaws for fracture control.</p>","33.11":"<p>Large aluminum–lithium tanks are joined by <em>friction stir welding</em>, a solid-state process that avoids the porosity and hot cracking of fusion welds. Domes are spin-formed, and integral stiffening is machined or chemically milled. Composite structures use automated fiber placement and, increasingly, out-of-autoclave cures. <em>Additive manufacturing</em> (laser powder-bed fusion of nickel and copper alloys; directed-energy deposition for large nozzles) consolidates injectors, chambers and turbomachinery parts into fewer pieces. Each process needs qualification, because porosity, surface roughness and residual stress change fatigue life and heat transfer; hot isostatic pressing and machining are common remedies. Nondestructive evaluation (radiography and CT, ultrasonics, eddy current, penetrant; thermography and shearography for composites) must reliably find flaws smaller than the critical sizes set by fracture control (Chapter 20). Quality systems add process control, material traceability, first-article inspection, configuration management and foreign-object-debris control.</p>"}},"34":{"intro":"","secs":{"34.1":"<p>A typical vehicle carries redundant flight computers, an inertial measurement unit (IMU), GNSS receivers, telemetry transmitters and command receivers, power distribution and batteries, actuator controllers, and an independent flight termination system. Data buses include MIL-STD-1553B (1 Mbit/s, deterministic, dual-redundant), SpaceWire, CAN and Ethernet variants (including time-triggered Ethernet). Harness mass and electromagnetic compatibility are significant design drivers.</p>","34.2":"<p>A gyro measures <span class=\"mi\">\\widetilde{\\mathbf{\\omega}} = \\left( \\mathbf{I} + \\mathbf{S} + \\mathbf{M} \\right)\\mathbf{\\omega} + \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{n}</span>: scale factor, misalignment, bias and noise (accelerometers analogously). Key specifications are bias stability, <em>angle random walk</em> (gyro white noise, °/√h) and <em>velocity random walk</em> (accelerometer white noise). The <em>Allan variance</em> versus averaging time separates the noise types: slope −1/2 indicates white noise, a flat floor indicates bias instability, and slope +1/2 indicates rate random walk. Bias stability spans from about 0.01°/h (navigation grade) to 1–10°/h (tactical) and 10°/h or worse (consumer MEMS).</p>","34.3":"<p>The IMU is fixed to the body. Integrate attitude from the gyros (quaternions with coning compensation, Chapter 32); rotate the measured specific force to the navigation frame; add a gravity model; then integrate to velocity and position.</p><div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Error growth.</strong> An uncorrected accelerometer bias <span class=\"mi\">b</span> gives a position error of about <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{1}{2}bt^{2}</span> at short times. A gyro bias <span class=\"mi\">\\varepsilon</span> tilts the platform and gives about <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{1}{6}g\\varepsilon t^{3}</span>. Over longer times, horizontal errors oscillate at the <strong>Schuler period</strong> <span class=\"mi\">2\\pi\\sqrt{R/g} = 84.4</span> min instead of growing without bound. The vertical channel is unstable and needs external aiding (altimeter or GNSS).</p></div>","34.4":"<p>A pseudorange is <span class=\"mi\">\\rho = \\left| \\mathbf{r}_{sat} - \\mathbf{r} \\right| + c\\,\\delta t_{r} + \\text{errors}</span>. With four or more satellites, least squares (Chapter 10) solves for position and receiver clock offset. The covariance is <span class=\"mi\">\\sigma^{2}\\left( \\mathbf{G}^{T}\\mathbf{G} \\right)^{- 1}</span>, where <span class=\"mi\">\\mathbf{G}</span> is the geometry matrix; its trace terms define the <em>dilution of precision</em> (PDOP, GDOP). Error sources are ionospheric and tropospheric delay, multipath, and satellite clock and ephemeris errors. Carrier-phase processing reaches centimeters. GNSS is usable well above its own constellation by tracking signal sidelobes. <em>GNSS/INS integration</em> may be loosely coupled (position fixes), tightly coupled (pseudoranges) or ultra-tightly coupled (inside the tracking loops), each implemented as a Kalman filter (Chapter 46).</p><aside class=\"flag\"><p><strong>Safety and scope flag · Export control (scope note)</strong></p><p><strong>Concern.</strong> High-performance inertial sensors, GNSS receivers able to operate at high speed and altitude, and certain navigation algorithms and data are export-controlled because they enable missile guidance.</p><p><strong>Why it is flagged.</strong> Even sharing controlled technical data with a foreign person can count as an export (Chapter 54). The principles in this chapter are openly taught; specific controlled hardware and data are not.</p><p><strong>Omitted here.</strong> Specifications and designs of controlled navigation hardware and software.</p><p><strong>Learn it safely.</strong> University navigation courses and the open textbooks in Appendix K (Groves; Titterton and Weston); the Institute of Navigation (ION) and its conferences; work with controlled hardware at employers with export-compliance programs.</p></aside>","34.5":"<div class=\"key\"><p><strong>Link equation (decibels):</strong> <span class=\"mi\">\\frac{E_{b}}{N_{0}} = EIRP - L_{fs} - L_{other} + \\frac{G}{T} + 228.6 - 10\\log_{10}R_{b}</span>, where 228.6 dB is <span class=\"mi\">- 10\\log_{10}k</span> and <span class=\"mi\">R_{b}</span> is the bit rate. The <em>margin</em> is achieved minus required <span class=\"mi\">E_{b}/N_{0}</span>; 3 dB or more is typical.</p></div><p>Required <span class=\"mi\">E_{b}/N_{0}</span> depends on modulation and coding: uncoded BPSK needs about 9.6 dB for a bit error rate of <span class=\"mi\">10^{- 5}</span>, while modern LDPC and turbo codes need a few dB. Shannon’s limit, <span class=\"mi\">C = B\\log_{2}(1 + S/N)</span>, implies <span class=\"mi\">E_{b}/N_{0} \\geq - 1.6</span> dB. Bands: S (2–4 GHz), X (8–12 GHz), Ka (26–40 GHz). Antenna beamwidth is roughly <span class=\"mi\">70\\lambda/D</span> degrees, so high gain needs pointing. Telemetry formats follow CCSDS standards. Tracking uses range and Doppler from ground networks and relay satellites.</p>","34.6":"<p>Flight software runs on a real-time operating system with deterministic scheduling. Under <em>rate-monotonic</em> priority (faster tasks get higher priority), <span class=\"mi\">n</span> periodic tasks are guaranteed schedulable if CPU utilization stays below <span class=\"mi\">n\\left( 2^{1/n} - 1 \\right)</span>, about 69% for large <span class=\"mi\">n</span>. Mode logic is written as explicit state machines. <em>Fault detection, isolation and recovery</em> (FDIR) uses watchdog timers and safe modes. Redundancy may be dual (detects a fault), triple with voting (masks one fault), or Byzantine-tolerant (<span class=\"mi\">3f + 1</span> units for <span class=\"mi\">f</span> arbitrary faults).</p><div class=\"pit\"><p><strong>Common-mode failure.</strong> Identical redundant units running identical software fail together. On Ariane 5’s first flight (1996), both inertial reference units ran reused software that hit the same unprotected numeric overflow, because the new trajectory produced values the old vehicle never had. Requirements must be re-validated whenever software is reused.</p></div><p>Assurance standards (NASA NPR 7150.2; DO-178C, which requires MC/DC structural coverage at its highest level) define unit, integration and hardware-in-the-loop testing, static analysis, configuration control and, increasingly, formal methods.</p>","34.7":"<p>Effects include <em>total ionizing dose</em> (krad(Si)) and displacement damage, which degrade parts gradually, and <em>single-event effects</em>: upsets (bit flips), latch-up (potentially destructive; requires current limiting and power cycling), transients and functional interrupts. The radiation comes from the trapped belts (including the South Atlantic Anomaly), solar particle events and galactic cosmic rays. Mitigation: radiation-hardened parts, error-detecting-and-correcting memory with scrubbing, triple modular redundancy in FPGAs, shielding, and latch-up protection. Screened commercial parts serve short or low-orbit missions.</p>","34.8":"<p>Plasma around an entering vehicle blocks radio frequencies below the plasma frequency (Chapter 48), so navigation through blackout relies on inertial sensors alone. RF windows (silica-based composites, silicon nitride) must survive heating. Antennas are placed where electron density is lowest, often in the wake. Electronics must be thermally isolated and survive intense boundary-layer-induced vibration.</p>"}},"35":{"intro":"","secs":{"35.1":"<p>Systems engineering ensures that the integrated system meets the stakeholders’ needs throughout its life. NASA’s phases: Pre-A (concept studies), A (concept and technology development), B (preliminary design), C (final design and fabrication), D (assembly, integration, test and launch), E (operations), F (closeout). Major reviews gate progress: Mission Concept, System Requirements, System Definition, Preliminary Design (PDR), Critical Design (CDR), System Integration, Test Readiness, Operational Readiness and Flight Readiness. The <em>V-model</em> pairs each level of decomposition on the way down with a level of integration and verification on the way up.</p>","35.2":"<p>Requirements form a hierarchy (mission, system, subsystem, component) and are written as “shall” statements. Each must be necessary, unambiguous, verifiable, feasible, singular and traceable to a parent need, with a verification method assigned. Interfaces are defined in interface control documents. Unresolved values (TBD/TBR) are tracked and burned down. Uncontrolled requirement growth is a leading cause of cost overruns.</p>","35.3":"<p>Decompose functions, map them to physical elements and capture interactions (N² diagrams, or SysML models in model-based systems engineering, Chapter 50). A trade study defines alternatives, figures of merit, weights and scores. Always test the sensitivity of the answer to the weights, discard dominated options, and record the rationale for the decision.</p>","35.4":"<p>Budgets cover mass, power, Δv and propellant, link, pointing, data, thermal and cost. Mass-growth allowances shrink as maturity rises, from roughly 20–30% at concept to a few percent at CDR. <em>Define margin explicitly</em>: “25% margin” may mean 25% of the current best estimate or 25% of the allowable, and confusing the two has caused real problems.</p>","35.5":"<p>Failure rates follow a <em>bathtub curve</em>: infant mortality (screened by burn-in and acceptance testing), a useful life with constant <span class=\"mi\">\\lambda</span>, then wear-out. Analysis tools:</p><ul> <li><strong>FMEA/FMECA</strong> (bottom-up): for each failure mode of each part, record its effect, severity, likelihood and detectability.</li> <li><strong>Fault tree analysis</strong> (top-down): the undesired top event decomposes through AND/OR gates down to basic events. For independent rare events, <span class=\"mi\">P\\left( \\text{OR} \\right) \\approx \\sum P_{i}</span> and <span class=\"mi\">P\\left( \\text{AND} \\right) = \\prod P_{i}</span>; minimal cut sets identify the critical combinations.</li> <li><strong>Probabilistic risk assessment</strong> combines event trees and fault trees into quantitative risk.</li> </ul><p><em>Common-cause failures</em> defeat redundancy and are modeled explicitly (for example with a β-factor). Single-point failures need special justification. Demonstrated reliability from a few flights is statistically weak; estimate it with Bayesian methods (Chapter 13).</p>","35.6":"<p><em>Verification</em> asks “did we build it right?” (does it meet its requirements?); <em>validation</em> asks “did we build the right thing?” (does it meet the need?). Methods are test, analysis with validated models, inspection and demonstration. Prefer test for critical functions: “test like you fly, fly like you test.” Qualification demonstrates design margin, acceptance screens workmanship, and protoflight combines the two on flight hardware. The environmental campaign covers vibration, acoustics, shock, thermal vacuum and EMI/EMC, followed by end-to-end system tests. Every anomaly is investigated to root cause and closed.</p>","35.7":"<div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>TRL</th> <th>Meaning</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>1–2</td> <td>Basic principles observed; concept formulated</td> </tr> <tr> <td>3</td> <td>Analytical or experimental proof of concept</td> </tr> <tr> <td>4</td> <td>Component validated in the laboratory</td> </tr> <tr> <td>5</td> <td>Component validated in a relevant environment</td> </tr> <tr> <td>6</td> <td>System or subsystem prototype demonstrated in a relevant environment</td> </tr> <tr> <td>7</td> <td>Prototype demonstrated in the operational (space) environment</td> </tr> <tr> <td>8</td> <td>Actual system completed and flight qualified</td> </tr> <tr> <td>9</td> <td>Actual system flight proven in operation</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div><p>Risk is scored as likelihood × consequence (for example on a 5 × 5 matrix) and reduced by planned mitigation with a “burn-down” schedule. Immature technology (below TRL 6 at PDR) is the classic root of cost and schedule overruns.</p>","35.8":"<p>Hazard analysis proceeds from a preliminary hazard list through system hazard analyses. Controls follow a hierarchy: eliminate the hazard; design for minimum hazard; add safety devices; add warnings; rely on procedures and training last. Crewed systems typically require two-fault tolerance against catastrophic hazards.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table> <thead> <tr> <th>Event</th> <th>Year</th> <th>Lesson</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Apollo 1 fire</td> <td>1967</td> <td>Pure oxygen at high pressure, flammable materials and an inward-opening hatch: test hazards are flight hazards</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Challenger</td> <td>1986</td> <td>O-ring erosion had been seen and gradually accepted (<em>normalization of deviance</em>); cold-temperature data and engineering concerns were overridden</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Ariane 5, flight 501</td> <td>1996</td> <td>Reused software in a new flight envelope; unhandled overflow in redundant units</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Mars Climate Orbiter</td> <td>1999</td> <td>Units mismatch across an interface</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Mars Polar Lander</td> <td>1999</td> <td>Likely spurious touchdown signal from leg deployment shut the engine down early</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Columbia</td> <td>2003</td> <td>Foam strike damaged the wing leading edge; organizational culture discounted the risk</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Genesis</td> <td>2004</td> <td>Deceleration sensors installed in the wrong orientation; parachute did not deploy</td> </tr> </tbody> </table></div>","35.9":"<p>Parametric cost models relate cost to mass, complexity and heritage. <em>Learning curves:</em> the cost of the <span class=\"mi\">n</span>th unit is <span class=\"mi\">T_{1}n^{\\log_{2}LC}</span>; with a 90% learning curve, each doubling of quantity cuts unit cost by 10%. Schedules are managed through the critical path; earned-value metrics (cost and schedule performance indices) track progress. Reusability changes the economics through refurbishment cost and flight rate.</p>"}}}});